{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K4qvVAkT3DHuXJrtEJGf56","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"A proof object for the customer search (plan 3 §1 Q1b, loop0004 item 05).\n2\t\n3\tThe complete customer search (`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `.c`)\n4\trefutes \"MOSP(I) ≤ k?\" by exhausting a tree of closing orders pruned by Chu &\n5\tStuckey's dominance rules, and until now left nothing behind that anyone could\n6\tcheck: two false refutations survived every audit because every audit only\n7\tconfirmed that values are *achievable* (`reports/better_move_bug.md`). This\n8\tmodule defines a **search certificate** -- the tree a refutation leaves, with\n9\tevery pruning decision named by its rule and its witness -- an **emitter** that\n10\trecords one from an instrumented copy of the search, and an **independent\n11\tchecker** that verifies every premise from the instance and the state and\n12\tconfirms that the tree is exhaustive, without running any dominance rule of\n13\tits own.\n14\t\n15\t**The certificate.** One record per search node, in the order the search\n16\tvisited them. A node carries the customer whose closing reached it (`move`),\n17\tthe free moves it closed (`free`), its kind (`refuted`, `memo` citing an earlier\n18\tnode with the same closed set, `sat` or `aborted`, which are not refutations),\n19\tits pruning `steps` and its `children`. A step is one of\n20\t\n21\t    [\"definite\", q]        Theorem 1: q keeps, every other candidate goes;\n22\t                           witness: q, checked as close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)\n23\t    [\"subset\", r, d]       the subset rule: r goes, d dominates it;\n24\t                           witness: d, checked as o(d,S) ⊆ o(r,S) with the\n25\t                           index tie-break on equality\n26\t    [\"better\", r, q]       Theorem 2: r goes, q covers it; witness: q, checked\n27\t                           as S++[q] and S++[r,q] playable in the paper's\n28\t                           measure and close(q, S∪{r}) ≥ open(q, S∪{r}) with\n29\t                           the corrected count (`reports/better_move_bug.md` §7)\n30\t\n31\tThe cost cut and Theorem 3 (old move) record nothing: the checker recomputes\n32\tboth -- the cost of every candidate from the state, and the set Q(S) of moves\n33\tan ancestor already searched from the *path*, with the reinsertion test the\n34\tsearch makes at each descent. The closed set of a node is likewise derived by\n35\tthe checker from the path, so a certificate is the tree and the witnesses and\n36\tnothing else.\n37\t\n38\t**What the checker verifies at each node**, given the state it derived:\n39\t\n40\t1. the listed free moves are exactly the remaining customers whose\n41\t   neighbourhood is already opened;\n42\t2. the node is not a solution (closed ≠ full);\n43\t3. a `memo` node cites an earlier *refuted* node with the same closed set,\n44\t   and only when old move is off (see below);\n45\t4. every premise of every step holds, recomputed from the instance and the\n46\t   state;\n47\t5. every candidate with cost ≤ k is a child, or is covered by a step, and\n48\t   every chain of coverings ends at a child or -- under old move -- at a\n49\t   customer in Q(S), with no cycle;\n50\t6. the children, recursively, with Q inherited by the reinsertion test.\n51\t\n52\tStep 5 is what the two historical bugs violate: the 2026-09-26 cross-rule\n53\tcycle has every step's premise true and no chain ending anywhere, and the\n54\tclose-count bug has a `better` step whose premise the checker recomputes as\n55\tfalse. Both are rejected (`tests/test_search_certificate.py`).\n56\t\n57\t**Which steps are locally checkable.** The subset rule, Theorem 1 and Theorem\n58\t2 are checkable from the instance and the state alone, step by step -- every\n59\tquantity in their premises is a function of `(N, S, O(S), k, r, q)`. What is\n60\t*not* a property of one step is its termination: a Theorem 2 step \"r goes\n61\tbecause q covers it\" is sound only if q is itself explored or covered by a\n62\tchain that ends in something explored, and that is a property of the whole\n63\tstep set at the node (the checker's step 5). Theorem 3 is checkable from the\n64\t*path*, not the state: Q(S) is which siblings an ancestor searched, threaded\n65\tthrough the reinsertion tests. The memo is checkable from the state alone only\n66\twithout old move: with it, a refuted state's subtree may rest on Q entries\n67\tinherited from ancestors above the cited node, so the refutation belongs to\n68\tthe path and a second path reaching the same set has no right to it. The\n69\temitter never combines the two, as the Python reference does not; the C does\n70\t(Chu & Stuckey run both), and a certificate of the C's run in that\n71\tconfiguration would need the checker to re-derive the cited subtree under the\n72\tnew path's Q -- which is a replay, not a lookup. That is the precise form of\n73\tthe kill criterion's answer, and it concerns Theorem 3 with the memo, not\n74\tTheorem 2.\n75\t\n76\t**What the checker trusts.** The three theorems themselves, in the free-move\n77\tcost model this search uses (Theorem 2's hypotheses as re-derived in\n78\t`reports/better_move_bug.md` §7 and checked there by brute force over 124 M\n79\tapplications), and the cost cut. It trusts neither the search nor the emitter:\n80\ta certificate that checks is a refutation whether or not the program that\n81\tproduced it was correct. This is the same division as a DRAT proof, which\n82\ttrusts resolution and not the solver.\n83\t\n84\t**The emitter** is a copy of `decide(native=False)`'s search with the rule\n85\twitnesses recorded, extended by a Python port of the C's `better_move` (today's\n86\trule: subset rule first over the remaining customers, Theorem 2 last over its\n87\tsurvivors, corrected close count) so that Theorem 2 steps can be certified at\n88\tall -- the Python reference never had the rule. Two measured reverts,\n89\t`old_close_count` and `old_rule_order`, reproduce the bugs for the tests. The\n90\temitter agrees with `decide(native=False)` in status and node count on every\n91\tconfiguration it shares, and with the C under `csearch` (`better_move=True`,\n92\t`better_move_dominators=0`) -- `tests/test_search_certificate.py`. It certifies\n93\tthe search under the rules **as Chu & Stuckey publish them**, i.e.\n94\t`decide(..., repaired_rules=False)`; the repaired rules became the default on\n95\t2026-10-01 (loop0007 item 03) and the emitter does not model them yet.\n96\t\n97\tRun:\n98\t\n99\t    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage run --max-customers 20 --workers 16\n100\t    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage run --max-customers 40 --min-customers 21 --workers 16 --deadline 60\n101\t    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage tables\n102\t    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage one --instance <name> --k <k>   # emit, check, print sizes\n103\t\n104\tWrites `learning/data/ensemble/search_certificate.csv` (one row per instance ×\n105\tconfiguration: status, nodes, steps by rule, bytes raw and gzipped, emit and\n106\tcheck seconds, the checker's verdict, the DRAT verdict of §17 and the lattice\n107\toracle's) and `reports/search_certificate_tables.md`. Nothing is written to\n108\t`solutions/`; no solver default is touched; nothing here decides `k` for\n109\tanything.\n110\t\"\"\"\n111\t\n112\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n113\t\n114\timport argparse\n115\timport gzip\n116\timport hashlib\n117\timport json\n118\timport multiprocessing\n119\timport time\n120\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n121\tfrom pathlib import Path\n122\t\n123\timport numpy as np\n124\timport pandas as pd\n125\t\n126\tfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n127\t\n128\tDATA_DIR = Path(\"learning/data/ensemble\")\n129\tDEFAULT_CSV = DATA_DIR / \"search_certificate.csv\"\n130\tDEFAULT_OUT = Path(\"reports/search_certificate_tables.md\")\n131\tPROOFS_CSV = Path(\"learning/data/proofs.csv\")\n132\tDEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR = Path(\"benchmarks/instances\")\n133\tDEFAULT_SOLUTIONS_DIR = Path(\"solutions\")\n134\t\n135\tCONFIGS = (\"default\", \"csearch\", \"memo\")\n136\tRULES = (\"definite\", \"subset\", \"better\")\n137\tSIZE_BANDS = ((0, 10), (11, 15), (16, 20), (21, 25), (26, 30), (31, 35), (36, 40),\n138\t              (41, 200))\n139\t\n140\t\n141\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n142\t# instance digest and masks -- the checker's own, sharing nothing with the search\n143\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n144\t\n145\t\n146\tdef matrix_sha256(instance: MOSPInstance) -> str:\n147\t    matrix = np.asarray(instance.matrix, dtype=np.int8)\n148\t    return hashlib.sha256(matrix.tobytes() + bytes(matrix.shape)).hexdigest()\n149\t\n150\t\n151\tdef neighbourhoods(instance: MOSPInstance) -> list[int]:\n152\t    \"\"\"Self-inclusive neighbourhoods N[c] as bitmasks, from the matrix alone.\"\"\"\n153\t    matrix = np.asarray(instance.matrix)\n154\t    n, m = matrix.shape\n155\t    masks = [0] * n\n156\t    for p in range(m):\n157\t        holders = np.flatnonzero(matrix[:, p])\n158\t        mask = 0\n159\t        for c in holders:\n160\t            mask |= 1 << int(c)\n161\t        for c in holders:\n162\t            masks[int(c)] |= mask\n163\t    return masks\n164\t\n165\t\n166\tdef _bits(mask: int):\n167\t    while mask:\n168\t        low = mask & -mask\n169\t        yield low.bit_length() - 1\n170\t        mask ^= low\n171\t\n172\t\n173\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n174\t# the certificate\n175\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n176\t\n177\t\n178\t@dataclass\n179\tclass Node:\n180\t    id: int\n181\t    parent: int | None\n182\t    move: int | None\n183\t    free: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n184\t    kind: str = \"refuted\"          # refuted | memo | sat | aborted\n185\t    memo_of: int | None = None\n186\t    steps: list[list[int | str]] = field(default_factory=list)\n187\t    children: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n188\t\n189\t    def to_json(self) -> dict:\n190\t        out = {\"id\": self.id, \"parent\": self.parent, \"move\": self.move}\n191\t        if self.free:\n192\t            out[\"free\"] = self.free\n193\t        if self.kind != \"refuted\":\n194\t            out[\"kind\"] = self.kind\n195\t        if self.memo_of is not None:\n196\t            out[\"memo_of\"] = self.memo_of\n197\t        if self.steps:\n198\t            out[\"steps\"] = self.steps\n199\t        if self.children:\n200\t            out[\"children\"] = self.children\n201\t        return out\n202\t\n203\t    @staticmethod\n204\t    def from_json(data: dict) -> \"Node\":\n205\t        return Node(id=int(data[\"id\"]), parent=data.get(\"parent\"), move=data.get(\"move\"),\n206\t                    free=list(data.get(\"free\", [])), kind=data.get(\"kind\", \"refuted\"),\n207\t                    memo_of=data.get(\"memo_of\"), steps=list(data.get(\"steps\", [])),\n208\t                    children=list(data.get(\"children\", [])))\n209\t\n210\t\n211\t@dataclass\n212\tclass Certificate:\n213\t    instance_name: str\n214\t    n_customers: int\n215\t    n_patterns: int\n216\t    matrix_sha256: str\n217\t    k: int\n218\t    config: dict\n219\t    status: str                    # unsat | sat | unknown\n220\t    nodes: list[Node]\n221\t    branches: int                  # the search's node count (branch decisions)\n222\t    order: list[int] | None = None\n223\t    emit_seconds: float = 0.0\n224\t\n225\t    def to_json(self) -> dict:\n226\t        return {\"format\": \"mosp-search-certificate-1\",\n227\t                \"instance_name\": self.instance_name, \"n_customers\": self.n_customers,\n228\t                \"n_patterns\": self.n_patterns, \"matrix_sha256\": self.matrix_sha256,\n229\t                \"k\": self.k, \"config\": self.config, \"status\": self.status,\n230\t                \"branches\": self.branches, \"order\": self.order,\n231\t                \"nodes\": [node.to_json() for node in self.nodes]}\n232\t\n233\t    def dumps(self) -> bytes:\n234\t        return json.dumps(self.to_json(), separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode()\n235\t\n236\t    @staticmethod\n237\t    def loads(data: bytes | str) -> \"Certificate\":\n238\t        obj = json.loads(data)\n239\t        if obj.get(\"format\") != \"mosp-search-certificate-1\":\n240\t            raise ValueError(\"not a search certificate\")\n241\t        return Certificate(instance_name=obj[\"instance_name\"], n_customers=obj[\"n_customers\"],\n242\t                           n_patterns=obj[\"n_patterns\"], matrix_sha256=obj[\"matrix_sha256\"],\n243\t                           k=int(obj[\"k\"]), config=dict(obj[\"config\"]), status=obj[\"status\"],\n244\t                           nodes=[Node.from_json(d) for d in obj[\"nodes\"]],\n245\t                           branches=int(obj[\"branches\"]), order=obj.get(\"order\"))\n246\t\n247\t    def step_counts(self) -> dict[str, int]:\n248\t        counts = {rule: 0 for rule in RULES}\n249\t        for node in self.nodes:\n250\t            for step in node.steps:\n251\t                counts[str(step[0])] += 1\n252\t        counts[\"memo\"] = sum(1 for node in self.nodes if node.kind == \"memo\")\n253\t        counts[\"free\"] = sum(len(node.free) for node in self.nodes)\n254\t        return counts\n255\t\n256\t    def sizes(self) -> dict[str, int]:\n257\t        raw = self.dumps()\n258\t        return {\"nodes\": len(self.nodes), \"steps\": sum(len(n.steps) for n in self.nodes),\n259\t                \"bytes\": len(raw), \"gz_bytes\": len(gzip.compress(raw, 9))}\n260\t\n261\t\n262\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n263\t# the emitter: decide(native=False) with witnesses, plus the C's better move\n264\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n265\t\n266\t\n267\tdef emit(\n268\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n269\t    k: int,\n270\t    *,\n271\t    subset_rule: bool = True,\n272\t    definite_move: bool = True,\n273\t    old_move: bool = True,\n274\t    memo: bool = False,\n275\t    better_move: bool = False,\n276\t    better_move_dominators: int = 0,\n277\t    old_close_count: bool = False,\n278\t    old_rule_order: bool = False,\n279\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n280\t    deadline: float | None = None,\n281\t) -> Certificate:\n282\t    \"\"\"Search \"MOSP(instance) ≤ k?\" as `decide(native=False)` does, recording the tree.\n283\t\n284\t    `memo` with `old_move` is refused, as the Python reference refuses it. With\n285\t    `better_move` the filter follows today's C: definite move, subset rule over\n286\t    the remaining customers, Theorem 2 over the subset survivors with only the\n287\t    first `better_move_dominators` (0: all) as dominators. `old_close_count`\n288\t    and `old_rule_order` are the two 2026-09-26 bugs, for the tests.\n289\t    \"\"\"\n290\t    if old_move and memo:\n291\t        raise ValueError(\"memo cannot be combined with old_move in a certificate: \"\n292\t                         \"a refutation under old move belongs to its path\")\n293\t    started = time.monotonic()\n294\t    masks = neighbourhoods(instance)\n295\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if masks[c]]\n296\t    full = 0\n297\t    for c in active:\n298\t        full |= 1 << c\n299\t    config = {\"subset_rule\": subset_rule, \"definite_move\": definite_move, \"old_move\": old_move,\n300\t              \"memo\": memo, \"better_move\": better_move,\n301\t              \"better_move_dominators\": better_move_dominators,\n302\t              \"old_close_count\": old_close_count, \"old_rule_order\": old_rule_order}\n303\t\n304\t    nodes: list[Node] = []\n305\t    path: list[int] = []\n306\t    refuted: dict[int, int] = {}\n307\t    state = {\"branches\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n308\t\n309\t    def new_node(parent: int | None, move: int | None) -> Node:\n310\t        node = Node(id=len(nodes), parent=parent, move=move)\n311\t        nodes.append(node)\n312\t        return node\n313\t\n314\t    if not active:\n315\t        root = new_node(None, None)\n316\t        root.kind = \"sat\"\n317\t        return Certificate(instance.name, instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns,\n318\t                           matrix_sha256(instance), k, config, \"sat\", nodes, 0, [],\n319\t                           time.monotonic() - started)\n320\t\n321\t    def search(node: Node, closed: int, opened: int, seen: int) -> bool:\n322\t        mark = len(path)\n323\t        free = 0\n324\t        for c in _bits(full & ~closed):\n325\t            if masks[c] & ~opened == 0:\n326\t                free |= 1 << c\n327\t        for c in _bits(free):\n328\t            path.append(c)\n329\t        node.free = list(_bits(free))\n330\t        closed |= free\n331\t        if closed == full:\n332\t            node.kind = \"sat\"\n333\t            return True\n334\t        if memo and closed in refuted:\n335\t            node.kind = \"memo\"\n336\t            node.memo_of = refuted[closed]\n337\t            del path[mark:]\n338\t            return False\n339\t\n340\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n341\t        seen &= remaining\n342\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n343\t        opens = {c: masks[c] & ~opened for c in _bits(remaining)}\n344\t        playable = [(((opened | masks[c]) & ~closed).bit_count(), c) for c in _bits(candidates)]\n345\t        playable = [(cost, c) for cost, c in playable if cost <= k]\n346\t\n347\t        if playable:\n348\t            playable = _filter(playable, opens, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n349\t        playable.sort()\n350\t\n351\t        for _, c in playable:\n352\t            state[\"branches\"] += 1\n353\t            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"branches\"] > max_nodes:\n354\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n355\t            if (deadline is not None and state[\"branches\"] % 256 == 0\n356\t                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n357\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n358\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n359\t                node.kind = \"aborted\"\n360\t                del path[mark:]\n361\t                return False\n362\t            here = len(path)\n363\t            path.append(c)\n364\t            inherited = 0\n365\t            if old_move and seen:\n366\t                for q in _bits(seen):\n367\t                    cost = ((opened | masks[q] | masks[c]) & ~(closed | (1 << q))).bit_count()\n368\t                    if cost <= k:\n369\t                        inherited |= 1 << q\n370\t            child = new_node(node.id, c)\n371\t            node.children.append(child.id)\n372\t            if search(child, closed | (1 << c), opened | masks[c], inherited):\n373\t                return True\n374\t            del path[here:]\n375\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n376\t                node.kind = \"aborted\"\n377\t                del path[mark:]\n378\t                return False\n379\t            seen |= 1 << c\n380\t\n381\t        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"]:\n382\t            refuted[closed] = node.id\n383\t        del path[mark:]\n384\t        return False\n385\t\n386\t    root = new_node(None, None)\n387\t    found = search(root, 0, 0, 0)\n388\t    if found:\n389\t        status, order = \"sat\", list(path)\n390\t    elif state[\"aborted\"]:\n391\t        status, order = \"unknown\", None\n392\t    else:\n393\t        status, order = \"unsat\", None\n394\t    return Certificate(instance.name, instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns,\n395\t                       matrix_sha256(instance), k, config, status, nodes, state[\"branches\"],\n396\t                       order, time.monotonic() - started)\n397\t\n398\t\n399\tdef _filter(playable, opens, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full):\n400\t    \"\"\"The dominance filter with witnesses, in `decide`'s / the C's order.\"\"\"\n401\t    if config[\"definite_move\"]:\n402\t        for cost, q in playable:\n403\t            own = opens[q]\n404\t            if sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0) >= own.bit_count():\n405\t                node.steps.append([\"definite\", q])\n406\t                return [(cost, q)]\n407\t    if not config[\"better_move\"]:\n408\t        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n409\t            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n410\t        return playable\n411\t    if config[\"old_rule_order\"]:\n412\t        playable = _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n413\t        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n414\t            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n415\t    else:\n416\t        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n417\t            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n418\t        playable = _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n419\t    return playable\n420\t\n421\t\n422\tdef _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude):\n423\t    kept, steps = [], []\n424\t    for cost, c in playable:\n425\t        own = opens[c]\n426\t        dominator = None\n427\t        for d, other in opens.items():\n428\t            if d == c or (exclude >> d) & 1:\n429\t                continue\n430\t            if other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < c):\n431\t                dominator = d\n432\t                break\n433\t        if dominator is None:\n434\t            kept.append((cost, c))\n435\t        else:\n436\t            steps.append([\"subset\", c, dominator])\n437\t    if not kept:\n438\t        return playable\n439\t    node.steps.extend(steps)\n440\t    return kept\n441\t\n442\t\n443\tdef _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full):\n444\t    \"\"\"Theorem 2 as the C's `better_move_pass`: an earlier candidate may cover a later one.\"\"\"\n445\t    if len(playable) <= 1:\n446\t        return playable\n447\t    limit = config[\"better_move_dominators\"]\n448\t    limit = len(playable) if limit <= 0 or limit > len(playable) else limit\n449\t    survivors, steps = [], []\n450\t    for ri, (cost_r, r) in enumerate(playable):\n451\t        cover = None\n452\t        for qi in range(min(limit, ri)):\n453\t            q = playable[qi][1]\n454\t            if q == r:\n455\t                continue\n456\t            if _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, config[\"old_close_count\"], full):\n457\t                cover = q\n458\t                break\n459\t        if cover is None:\n460\t            survivors.append((cost_r, r))\n461\t        else:\n462\t            steps.append([\"better\", r, cover])\n463\t    node.steps.extend(steps)\n464\t    return survivors\n465\t\n466\t\n467\tdef _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, old_close_count=False, full=None) -> bool:\n468\t    \"\"\"close(q, S∪{r}) ≥ open(q, S∪{r}) with S++[r,q] playable in the paper's measure.\"\"\"\n469\t    if full is None:\n470\t        full = 0\n471\t        for c, mask in enumerate(masks):\n472\t            if mask:\n473\t                full |= 1 << c\n474\t    closed_r = closed | (1 << r)\n475\t    opened_r = opened | masks[r]\n476\t    if ((opened_r | masks[q]) & ~closed_r).bit_count() > k:\n477\t        return False\n478\t    own = masks[q] & ~opened_r\n479\t    opened_by = own.bit_count()\n480\t    closed_by = 0\n481\t    for d in _bits(full & ~closed_r):\n482\t        left = masks[d] & ~opened_r\n483\t        if (left or old_close_count) and left & ~own == 0:\n484\t            closed_by += 1\n485\t    return closed_by >= opened_by\n486\t\n487\t\n488\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n489\t# the checker\n490\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n491\t\n492\t\n493\t@dataclass\n494\tclass CheckResult:\n495\t    ok: bool\n496\t    reason: str\n497\t    nodes: int\n498\t    steps: int\n499\t    seconds: float\n500\t\n501\t\n502\tclass Rejected(Exception):\n503\t    pass\n504\t\n505\t\n506\tdef check(instance: MOSPInstance, cert: Certificate) -> CheckResult:\n507\t    \"\"\"Verify that `cert` is a refutation of \"MOSP(instance) ≤ k\" (see the module docstring).\"\"\"\n508\t    started = time.monotonic()\n509\t    counted = {\"nodes\": 0, \"steps\": 0}\n510\t    try:\n511\t        _check(instance, cert, counted)\n512\t    except Rejected as why:\n513\t        return CheckResult(False, str(why), counted[\"nodes\"], counted[\"steps\"],\n514\t                           time.monotonic() - started)\n515\t    return CheckResult(True, \"refutation verified\", counted[\"nodes\"], counted[\"steps\"],\n516\t                       time.monotonic() - started)\n517\t\n518\t\n519\tdef _check(instance: MOSPInstance, cert: Certificate, counted: dict) -> None:\n520\t    if cert.status != \"unsat\":\n521\t        raise Rejected(f\"certificate status is {cert.status!r}, not a refutation\")\n522\t    if cert.matrix_sha256 != matrix_sha256(instance):\n523\t        raise Rejected(\"matrix digest does not match the instance\")\n524\t    if (cert.n_customers, cert.n_patterns) != (instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns):\n525\t        raise Rejected(\"instance dimensions do not match\")\n526\t    config = cert.config\n527\t    old_move = bool(config.get(\"old_move\", False))\n528\t    memo_allowed = bool(config.get(\"memo\", False)) and not old_move\n529\t    if config.get(\"memo\") and old_move:\n530\t        raise Rejected(\"memo references are not checkable under old move: \"\n531\t                       \"a refuted state's subtree may rest on Q inherited along its path\")\n532\t    k = cert.k\n533\t    masks = neighbourhoods(instance)\n534\t    full = 0\n535\t    for c, mask in enumerate(masks):\n536\t        if mask:\n537\t            full |= 1 << c\n538\t    nodes = {node.id: node for node in cert.nodes}\n539\t    if len(nodes) != len(cert.nodes):\n540\t        raise Rejected(\"duplicate node ids\")\n541\t    if not cert.nodes or cert.nodes[0].parent is not None:\n542\t        raise Rejected(\"no root\")\n543\t    refuted_states: dict[int, int] = {}    # closed set -> node id, completed refutations\n544\t    completed: set[int] = set()\n545\t\n546\t    def visit(node: Node, closed: int, opened: int, seen: int) -> None:\n547\t        counted[\"nodes\"] += 1\n548\t        # 1. free moves: exactly the remaining customers whose N is opened\n549\t        computed_free = 0\n550\t        for c in _bits(full & ~closed):\n551\t            if masks[c] & ~opened == 0:\n552\t                computed_free |= 1 << c\n553\t        listed = 0\n554\t        for c in node.free:\n555\t            listed |= 1 << int(c)\n556\t        if listed != computed_free:\n557\t            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: free moves {node.free} are not the customers \"\n558\t                           f\"whose neighbourhood is opened\")\n559\t        closed |= computed_free\n560\t        # 2. not a solution\n561\t        if closed == full:\n562\t            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: every customer is closed -- the tree holds a solution\")\n563\t        if node.kind in (\"sat\", \"aborted\"):\n564\t            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: kind {node.kind!r} inside a refutation\")\n565\t        # 3. memo\n566\t        if node.kind == \"memo\":\n567\t            if not memo_allowed:\n568\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo reference without memo enabled\")\n569\t            cited = node.memo_of\n570\t            if cited is None or cited not in completed:\n571\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo cites node {cited}, not a completed refutation\")\n572\t            if refuted_states.get(closed) != cited:\n573\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo cites node {cited}, whose closed set differs\")\n574\t            if node.children or node.steps:\n575\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: a memo node carries children or steps\")\n576\t            return\n577\t        if node.kind != \"refuted\":\n578\t            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: unknown kind {node.kind!r}\")\n579\t\n580\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n581\t        seen &= remaining\n582\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n583\t        opens = {c: masks[c] & ~opened for c in _bits(remaining)}\n584\t        cost = {c: ((opened | masks[c]) & ~closed).bit_count() for c in _bits(candidates)}\n585\t        playable = {c for c, v in cost.items() if v <= k}\n586\t        children = []\n587\t        for cid in node.children:\n588\t            child = nodes.get(int(cid))\n589\t            if child is None or child.parent != node.id or child.move is None:\n590\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: child id {cid} missing or mis-parented\")\n591\t            children.append(int(child.move))\n592\t        child_set = set(children)\n593\t        if len(child_set) != len(children):\n594\t            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: repeated child\")\n595\t        for c in children:\n596\t            if c not in playable:\n597\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: child {c} is not a playable candidate\")\n598\t\n599\t        # 4. premises, building the covering relation\n600\t        cover: dict[int, int] = {}\n601\t        definite: int | None = None\n602\t        for step in node.steps:\n603\t            counted[\"steps\"] += 1\n604\t            rule = step[0]\n605\t            if rule == \"definite\":\n606\t                q = int(step[1])\n607\t                if q not in playable:\n608\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: definite move keeps {q}, not a playable candidate\")\n609\t                own = opens[q]\n610\t                closed_by = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n611\t                if closed_by < own.bit_count():\n612\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: definite move on {q}: close {closed_by} < open \"\n613\t                                   f\"{own.bit_count()}\")\n614\t                if definite is not None:\n615\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: two definite moves\")\n616\t                definite = q\n617\t                for r in playable:\n618\t                    if r != q:\n619\t                        cover.setdefault(r, q)\n620\t            elif rule == \"subset\":\n621\t                r, d = int(step[1]), int(step[2])\n622\t                if r not in playable or d not in opens or d == r:\n623\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: subset step ({r}, {d}) names a non-candidate\")\n624\t                other, own = opens[d], opens[r]\n625\t                if not (other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < r)):\n626\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: subset step ({r}, {d}): o({d}) is not a \"\n627\t                                   f\"(tie-broken) subset of o({r})\")\n628\t                if r in cover:\n629\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: {r} discarded twice\")\n630\t                cover[r] = d\n631\t            elif rule == \"better\":\n632\t                r, q = int(step[1]), int(step[2])\n633\t                if r not in playable or q not in playable or q == r:\n634\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: better step ({r}, {q}) names a non-candidate\")\n635\t                if not _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, full=full):\n636\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: better step ({r}, {q}): close(q, S∪{{r}}) < \"\n637\t                                   f\"open(q, S∪{{r}}) or S++[r,q] not playable\")\n638\t                if r in cover:\n639\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: {r} discarded twice\")\n640\t                cover[r] = q\n641\t            else:\n642\t                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: unknown rule {rule!r}\")\n643\t\n644\t        # 5. exhaustiveness: every playable candidate ends at a child or in Q(S)\n645\t        for c in sorted(playable):\n646\t            if c in child_set:\n647\t                continue\n648\t            here, trail = c, set()\n649\t            while True:\n650\t                if here in child_set:\n651\t                    break\n652\t                if old_move and (seen >> here) & 1:\n653\t                    break                      # Theorem 3: searched at an ancestor\n654\t                if here in trail:\n655\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: covering cycle through {sorted(trail)}\")\n656\t                trail.add(here)\n657\t                if here not in cover:\n658\t                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: candidate {here} (cost {cost.get(here)}) is \"\n659\t                                   f\"neither explored nor covered\")\n660\t                here = cover[here]\n661\t\n662\t        # 6. children in order, Q inherited by the reinsertion test\n663\t        for cid in node.children:\n664\t            child = nodes[int(cid)]\n665\t            c = int(child.move)\n666\t            inherited = 0\n667\t            if old_move and seen:\n668\t                for q in _bits(seen):\n669\t                    if ((opened | masks[q] | masks[c]) & ~(closed | (1 << q))).bit_count() <= k:\n670\t                        inherited |= 1 << q\n671\t            visit(child, closed | (1 << c), opened | masks[c], inherited)\n672\t            seen |= 1 << c\n673\t        completed.add(node.id)\n674\t        if memo_allowed:\n675\t            refuted_states[closed] = node.id\n676\t\n677\t    visit(cert.nodes[0], 0, 0, 0)\n678\t    if counted[\"nodes\"] != len(cert.nodes):\n679\t        raise Rejected(f\"{len(cert.nodes) - counted['nodes']} nodes are not reachable from the root\")\n680\t\n681\t\n682\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n683\t# the study: the corpus at n ≤ 20 (and beyond) against DRAT and the lattice\n684\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n685\t\n686\t\n687\tdef config_kwargs(instance: MOSPInstance, config: str) -> dict:\n688\t    \"\"\"The emitter's keyword arguments under a named configuration.\n689\t\n690\t    `default`: the Python reference (subset rule, Theorem 1, Theorem 3, no memo).\n691\t    `csearch`: the same with Theorem 2 where `sparse_enough_for_better_move`\n692\t    says so, every candidate a dominator -- the configuration that certified\n693\t    the corpus. `memo`: the subset rule, Theorem 1 and the memo, no Theorem 3 --\n694\t    the one configuration whose memo references the checker accepts.\n695\t    \"\"\"\n696\t    if config == \"default\":\n697\t        return {}\n698\t    if config == \"csearch\":\n699\t        from satisfiability.customer_search import sparse_enough_for_better_move\n700\t\n701\t        return {\"better_move\": sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance),\n702\t                \"better_move_dominators\": 0}\n703\t    if config == \"memo\":\n704\t        return {\"old_move\": False, \"memo\": True}\n705\t    raise ValueError(f\"unknown configuration {config!r}; one of {CONFIGS}\")\n706\t\n707\t\n708\tdef certify_refutation(instance: MOSPInstance, optimum: int, config: str,\n709\t                       deadline_seconds: float | None = None) -> dict:\n710\t    \"\"\"Emit at `optimum − 1`, check, and describe the certificate in one row.\"\"\"\n711\t    kwargs = config_kwargs(instance, config)\n712\t    deadline = None if deadline_seconds is None else time.monotonic() + deadline_seconds\n713\t    cert = emit(instance, optimum - 1, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n714\t    row = {\"config\": config, \"k\": optimum - 1, \"status\": cert.status, \"branches\": cert.branches,\n715\t           \"better_move\": bool(kwargs.get(\"better_move\", False)),\n716\t           \"emit_seconds\": round(cert.emit_seconds, 5)}\n717\t    row.update({f\"steps_{rule}\": v for rule, v in cert.step_counts().items()})\n718\t    row.update(cert.sizes())\n719\t    if cert.status == \"unsat\":\n720\t        result = check(instance, Certificate.loads(cert.dumps()))\n721\t        row.update(check_ok=result.ok, check_reason=result.reason,\n722\t                   check_seconds=round(result.seconds, 5))\n723\t    else:\n724\t        row.update(check_ok=None, check_reason=\"\", check_seconds=None)\n725\t    return row\n726\t\n727\t\n728\tdef _job(args) -> list[dict]:\n729\t    matrix, name, source_file, collection, optimum, configs, deadline = args\n730\t    instance = MOSPInstance(matrix=np.array(matrix, dtype=np.int8), n_customers=len(matrix),\n731\t                            n_patterns=len(matrix[0]), name=name)\n732\t    base = {\"instance_name\": name, \"source_file\": source_file, \"collection\": collection,\n733\t            \"n\": instance.n_customers, \"m\": instance.n_patterns, \"optimum\": int(optimum)}\n734\t    oracle = None\n735\t    try:\n736\t        from learning.differential import oracle_minima\n737\t        minima = oracle_minima(instance)\n738\t        if minima is not None:\n739\t            oracle = int(minima[\"oracle_search\"])\n740\t    except Exception:          # the oracle is a cross-check, never a blocker\n741\t        oracle = None\n742\t    rows = []\n743\t    for config in configs:\n744\t        row = dict(base)\n745\t        row.update(certify_refutation(instance, int(optimum), config, deadline))\n746\t        row[\"oracle_search\"] = oracle\n747\t        rows.append(row)\n748\t    return rows\n749\t\n750\t\n751\tdef targets(min_customers: int, max_customers: int, instance_dir: Path = DEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR,\n752\t            solutions_dir: Path = DEFAULT_SOLUTIONS_DIR, limit: int | None = None) -> list[tuple]:\n753\t    from learning.node_counts import certified_targets\n754\t\n755\t    jobs = [job for job in certified_targets(instance_dir, solutions_dir, max_customers, None)\n756\t            if len(job[0]) >= min_customers]\n757\t    return jobs[:limit] if limit is not None else jobs\n758\t\n759\t\n760\tdef run(jobs: list[tuple], configs: tuple[str, ...], workers: int, deadline: float | None,\n761\t        table: Path = DEFAULT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\n762\t    table.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n763\t    done: set[tuple[str, str, str]] = set()\n764\t    if table.exists():\n765\t        old = pd.read_csv(table)\n766\t        done = set(zip(old.instance_name, old.source_file, old.config))\n767\t    todo = [(m, name, src, coll, opt, tuple(c for c in configs if (name, src, c) not in done), deadline)\n768\t            for m, name, src, coll, opt, _ in jobs]\n769\t    todo = [job for job in todo if job[5]]\n770\t    print(f\"{len(todo)} instances to certify under {configs} on {workers} workers\", flush=True)\n771\t    header = not table.exists()\n772\t    started = time.monotonic()\n773\t    with multiprocessing.get_context(\"spawn\").Pool(workers) as pool:\n774\t        with table.open(\"a\") as handle:\n775\t            for i, rows in enumerate(pool.imap_unordered(_job, todo, chunksize=1), 1):\n776\t                frame = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n777\t                frame.to_csv(handle, header=header, index=False)\n778\t                header = False\n779\t                if i % 500 == 0:\n780\t                    print(f\"  {i}/{len(todo)} in {time.monotonic() - started:.0f} s\", flush=True)\n781\t    return pd.read_csv(table)\n782\t\n783\t\n784\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n785\t# tables\n786\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n787\t\n788\t\n789\tdef _band(n: int) -> str:\n790\t    for lo, hi in SIZE_BANDS:\n791\t        if lo <= n <= hi:\n792\t            return f\"{lo}–{hi}\" if hi < 200 else f\"{lo}+\"\n793\t    return \"?\"\n794\t\n795\t\n796\tdef _md(frame: pd.DataFrame, floatfmt: str = \".3g\") -> str:\n797\t    return frame.to_markdown(index=False, floatfmt=floatfmt)\n798\t\n799\t\n800\tdef join_verdicts(frame: pd.DataFrame, proofs_csv: Path = PROOFS_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\n801\t    \"\"\"Beside each row, §17's DRAT verdict and the lattice oracle's agreement.\"\"\"\n802\t    out = frame.copy()\n803\t    if proofs_csv.exists():\n804\t        proofs = pd.read_csv(proofs_csv)[[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\", \"verdict\", \"status\"]]\n805\t        proofs = proofs.rename(columns={\"verdict\": \"drat_verdict\", \"status\": \"drat_status\"})\n806\t        out = out.merge(proofs, on=[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\"], how=\"left\")\n807\t    else:\n808\t        out[\"drat_verdict\"] = None\n809\t        out[\"drat_status\"] = None\n810\t    out[\"oracle_agrees\"] = out.oracle_search.notna() & (out.oracle_search == out.optimum)\n811\t    out[\"oracle_disagrees\"] = out.oracle_search.notna() & (out.oracle_search != out.optimum)\n812\t    return out\n813\t\n814\t\n815\tdef summarise(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> dict[str, pd.DataFrame]:\n816\t    frame = join_verdicts(frame)\n817\t    frame[\"band\"] = frame.n.map(_band)\n818\t    frame[\"band_lo\"] = frame.n.map(lambda n: next(lo for lo, hi in SIZE_BANDS if lo <= n <= hi))\n819\t    tables: dict[str, pd.DataFrame] = {}\n820\t\n821\t    # verdicts per configuration\n822\t    rows = []\n823\t    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n824\t        unsat = part[part.status == \"unsat\"]\n825\t        drat = part[part.drat_verdict == \"verified\"]\n826\t        oracle = part[part.oracle_search.notna()]\n827\t        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"instances\": len(part),\n828\t                     \"refuted (unsat)\": len(unsat), \"unknown (deadline)\": int((part.status == \"unknown\").sum()),\n829\t                     \"sat below optimum\": int((part.status == \"sat\").sum()),\n830\t                     \"certificates verified\": int(unsat.check_ok.fillna(False).astype(bool).sum()),\n831\t                     \"certificates rejected\": int((~unsat.check_ok.fillna(True).astype(bool)).sum()),\n832\t                     \"DRAT verified (§17)\": len(drat),\n833\t                     \"both certificate and DRAT\": int(((unsat.check_ok == True) & (unsat.drat_verdict == \"verified\")).sum()),\n834\t                     \"certificate where DRAT censored\": int(((unsat.check_ok == True) & (unsat.drat_verdict != \"verified\")).sum()),\n835\t                     \"lattice oracle available\": len(oracle),\n836\t                     \"oracle agrees\": int(oracle.oracle_agrees.sum()),\n837\t                     \"oracle disagrees\": int(oracle.oracle_disagrees.sum())})\n838\t    tables[\"verdicts\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n839\t\n840\t    # size and time by band, per config\n841\t    rows = []\n842\t    for (config, lo, band), part in frame.groupby([\"config\", \"band_lo\", \"band\"]):\n843\t        unsat = part[(part.status == \"unsat\")]\n844\t        if unsat.empty:\n845\t            continue\n846\t        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"band\": band, \"instances\": len(unsat),\n847\t                     \"nodes median\": unsat.nodes.median(), \"nodes p90\": unsat.nodes.quantile(0.9),\n848\t                     \"nodes max\": unsat.nodes.max(),\n849\t                     \"steps median\": unsat.steps.median(), \"steps max\": unsat.steps.max(),\n850\t                     \"bytes median\": unsat.bytes.median(), \"bytes max\": unsat.bytes.max(),\n851\t                     \"gz bytes median\": unsat.gz_bytes.median(), \"gz bytes max\": unsat.gz_bytes.max(),\n852\t                     \"emit ms median\": 1000 * unsat.emit_seconds.median(),\n853\t                     \"check ms median\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.median(),\n854\t                     \"check ms p90\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.quantile(0.9),\n855\t                     \"check ms max\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.max(),\n856\t                     \"bytes / branch\": (unsat.bytes.sum() / max(1, unsat.branches.sum()))})\n857\t    tables[\"by_band\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows).sort_values([\"config\", \"band\"], key=lambda s: s if s.name == \"config\" else s.map(lambda b: int(str(b).split(\"–\")[0].rstrip(\"+\"))))\n858\t\n859\t    # steps by rule per config\n860\t    rows = []\n861\t    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n862\t        unsat = part[part.status == \"unsat\"]\n863\t        total_steps = max(1, unsat.steps.sum())\n864\t        row = {\"config\": config, \"refutations\": len(unsat), \"branches\": int(unsat.branches.sum()),\n865\t               \"nodes\": int(unsat.nodes.sum()), \"steps\": int(unsat.steps.sum())}\n866\t        for rule in (\"definite\", \"subset\", \"better\", \"memo\", \"free\"):\n867\t            col = f\"steps_{rule}\"\n868\t            row[rule] = int(unsat[col].sum()) if col in unsat else 0\n869\t        row[\"zero-step refutations\"] = int((unsat.steps == 0).sum())\n870\t        row[\"zero-branch refutations\"] = int((unsat.branches == 0).sum())\n871\t        row[\"steps / branch\"] = unsat.steps.sum() / max(1, unsat.branches.sum())\n872\t        rows.append(row)\n873\t    tables[\"rules\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n874\t\n875\t    # check time against size: seconds per node, per step\n876\t    rows = []\n877\t    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n878\t        unsat = part[(part.status == \"unsat\") & part.check_seconds.notna()]\n879\t        if unsat.empty:\n880\t            continue\n881\t        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"refutations\": len(unsat),\n882\t                     \"check s total\": unsat.check_seconds.sum(),\n883\t                     \"emit s total\": unsat.emit_seconds.sum(),\n884\t                     \"check / emit\": unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1e-9, unsat.emit_seconds.sum()),\n885\t                     \"µs per node\": 1e6 * unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1, unsat.nodes.sum()),\n886\t                     \"µs per step\": 1e6 * unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1, unsat.steps.sum()),\n887\t                     \"bytes total\": int(unsat.bytes.sum()), \"gz bytes total\": int(unsat.gz_bytes.sum())})\n888\t    tables[\"cost\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n889\t\n890\t    # against the DRAT proofs of §17: the same instances, size and check time\n891\t    rows = []\n892\t    proofs = pd.read_csv(PROOFS_CSV) if PROOFS_CSV.exists() else None\n893\t    if proofs is not None:\n894\t        cs = frame[(frame.config == \"csearch\") & (frame.status == \"unsat\") & (frame.check_ok == True)]\n895\t        both = cs.merge(proofs[proofs.verdict == \"verified\"][\n896\t            [\"instance_name\", \"source_file\", \"proof_gz_bytes\", \"proof_bytes\", \"proof_lemmas\",\n897\t             \"check_seconds\", \"solve_seconds\", \"conflicts\"]].rename(\n898\t                columns={\"check_seconds\": \"drat_check_seconds\", \"solve_seconds\": \"drat_solve_seconds\"}),\n899\t            on=[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\"], how=\"inner\")\n900\t        for (lo, band), part in both.groupby([\"band_lo\", \"band\"]):\n901\t            rows.append({\"band\": band, \"instances (both proofs)\": len(part),\n902\t                         \"certificate gz bytes median\": part.gz_bytes.median(),\n903\t                         \"DRAT gz bytes median\": part.proof_gz_bytes.median(),\n904\t                         \"gz ratio DRAT / certificate (median of ratios)\": (part.proof_gz_bytes / part.gz_bytes).median(),\n905\t                         \"certificate check ms median\": 1000 * part.check_seconds.median(),\n906\t                         \"DRAT check ms median\": 1000 * part.drat_check_seconds.median(),\n907\t                         \"check ratio DRAT / certificate (median)\": (part.drat_check_seconds / part.check_seconds.clip(lower=1e-6)).median(),\n908\t                         \"certificate gz total MB\": part.gz_bytes.sum() / 1e6,\n909\t                         \"DRAT gz total MB\": part.proof_gz_bytes.sum() / 1e6,\n910\t                         \"certificate check s total\": part.check_seconds.sum(),\n911\t                         \"DRAT check s total\": part.drat_check_seconds.sum(),\n912\t                         \"emit s total\": part.emit_seconds.sum(),\n913\t                         \"DRAT solve s total\": part.drat_solve_seconds.sum()})\n914\t    tables[\"against_drat\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n915\t\n916\t    # rejected or disagreeing rows, if any\n917\t    bad = frame[((frame.status == \"unsat\") & (frame.check_ok == False))\n918\t                | (frame.status == \"sat\") | frame.oracle_disagrees]\n919\t    tables[\"flagged\"] = bad[[\"instance_name\", \"config\", \"n\", \"m\", \"optimum\", \"status\", \"check_ok\",\n920\t                             \"check_reason\", \"oracle_search\", \"drat_verdict\"]].head(50)\n921\t    return tables\n922\t\n923\t\n924\tdef write_tables(frame: pd.DataFrame, out: Path = DEFAULT_OUT) -> None:\n925\t    tables = summarise(frame)\n926\t    parts = [\"# Search certificates: tables (loop0004 item 05)\", \"\",\n927\t             f\"*Generated by `python -m learning.search_certificate --stage tables` from \"\n928\t             f\"`{DEFAULT_CSV}`: {len(frame)} rows, {frame.instance_name.nunique()} instances at \"\n929\t             f\"{frame.n.min()}–{frame.n.max()} customers.*\", \"\"]\n930\t    titles = {\"verdicts\": \"Verdicts per configuration, against §17's DRAT proofs and the lattice oracle (n_active ≤ 15)\",\n931\t              \"by_band\": \"Certificate size and check time by size band (refutations only)\",\n932\t              \"rules\": \"Steps by rule\",\n933\t              \"cost\": \"Check cost against emit cost and size\",\n934\t              \"against_drat\": \"Against §17's DRAT proofs on the instances that have both (csearch configuration)\",\n935\t              \"flagged\": \"Rejected certificates, satisfiable calls below the optimum, oracle disagreements\"}\n936\t    for key, title in titles.items():\n937\t        parts += [f\"## {title}\", \"\", _md(tables[key]) if not tables[key].empty else \"*(none)*\", \"\"]\n938\t    out.write_text(\"\\n\".join(parts))\n939\t    print(f\"wrote {out}\")\n940\t\n941\t\n942\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n943\t# entry point\n944\t# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n945\t\n946\t\n947\tdef main() -> None:\n948\t    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split(\"\\n\\n\")[0])\n949\t    parser.add_argument(\"--stage\", choices=(\"run\", \"tables\", \"one\"), default=\"tables\")\n950\t    parser.add_argument(\"--configs\", default=\",\".join(CONFIGS))\n951\t    parser.add_argument(\"--min-customers\", type=int, default=0)\n952\t    parser.add_argument(\"--max-customers\", type=int, default=20)\n953\t    parser.add_argument(\"--workers\", type=int, default=16)\n954\t    parser.add_argument(\"--deadline\", type=float, default=None, help=\"seconds per emit call\")\n955\t    parser.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=None)\n956\t    parser.add_argument(\"--table\", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_CSV)\n957\t    parser.add_argument(\"--out\", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_OUT)\n958\t    parser.add_argument(\"--instance\", type=str, default=None)\n959\t    parser.add_argument(\"--k\", type=int, default=None)\n960\t    parser.add_argument(\"--save\", type=Path, default=None, help=\"write the certificate (gzip JSON)\")\n961\t    args = parser.parse_args()\n962\t    configs = tuple(c for c in args.configs.split(\",\") if c)\n963\t\n964\t    if args.stage == \"run\":\n965\t        jobs = targets(args.min_customers, args.max_customers, limit=args.limit)\n966\t        frame = run(jobs, configs, args.workers, args.deadline, args.table)\n967\t        write_tables(frame, args.out)\n968\t    elif args.stage == \"tables\":\n969\t        write_tables(pd.read_csv(args.table), args.out)\n970\t    else:\n971\t        from learning.dataset import enumerate_instances\n972\t\n973\t        found = None\n974\t        for _, inst in enumerate_instances(Path(DEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR)):\n975\t            if inst.name == args.instance:\n976\t                found = inst\n977\t                break\n978\t        if found is None:\n979\t            raise SystemExit(f\"no instance named {args.instance!r}\")\n980\t        for config in configs:\n981\t            cert = emit(found, args.k, **config_kwargs(found, config))\n982\t            result = check(found, cert) if cert.status == \"unsat\" else None\n983\t            print(config, cert.status, \"branches\", cert.branches, cert.sizes(), cert.step_counts(),\n984\t                  result)\n985\t            if args.save is not None:\n986\t                args.save.write_bytes(gzip.compress(cert.dumps()))\n987\t\n988\t\n989\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n990\t    main()\n991\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be74ee69-bdd1-4ca9-adfe-51cb483bfc8a","uuid":"4cb4bcc6-f83a-41de-9d6b-e8fb4cd11bba","timestamp":"2026-10-03T04:33:29.907Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/learning/search_certificate.py","content":"\"\"\"A proof object for the customer search (plan 3 §1 Q1b, loop0004 item 05).\n\nThe complete customer search (`satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `.c`)\nrefutes \"MOSP(I) ≤ k?\" by exhausting a tree of closing orders pruned by Chu &\nStuckey's dominance rules, and until now left nothing behind that anyone could\ncheck: two false refutations survived every audit because every audit only\nconfirmed that values are *achievable* (`reports/better_move_bug.md`). This\nmodule defines a **search certificate** -- the tree a refutation leaves, with\nevery pruning decision named by its rule and its witness -- an **emitter** that\nrecords one from an instrumented copy of the search, and an **independent\nchecker** that verifies every premise from the instance and the state and\nconfirms that the tree is exhaustive, without running any dominance rule of\nits own.\n\n**The certificate.** One record per search node, in the order the search\nvisited them. A node carries the customer whose closing reached it (`move`),\nthe free moves it closed (`free`), its kind (`refuted`, `memo` citing an earlier\nnode with the same closed set, `sat` or `aborted`, which are not refutations),\nits pruning `steps` and its `children`. A step is one of\n\n    [\"definite\", q]        Theorem 1: q keeps, every other candidate goes;\n                           witness: q, checked as close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)\n    [\"subset\", r, d]       the subset rule: r goes, d dominates it;\n                           witness: d, checked as o(d,S) ⊆ o(r,S) with the\n                           index tie-break on equality\n    [\"better\", r, q]       Theorem 2: r goes, q covers it; witness: q, checked\n                           as S++[q] and S++[r,q] playable in the paper's\n                           measure and close(q, S∪{r}) ≥ open(q, S∪{r}) with\n                           the corrected count (`reports/better_move_bug.md` §7)\n\nThe cost cut and Theorem 3 (old move) record nothing: the checker recomputes\nboth -- the cost of every candidate from the state, and the set Q(S) of moves\nan ancestor already searched from the *path*, with the reinsertion test the\nsearch makes at each descent. The closed set of a node is likewise derived by\nthe checker from the path, so a certificate is the tree and the witnesses and\nnothing else.\n\n**What the checker verifies at each node**, given the state it derived:\n\n1. the listed free moves are exactly the remaining customers whose\n   neighbourhood is already opened;\n2. the node is not a solution (closed ≠ full);\n3. a `memo` node cites an earlier *refuted* node with the same closed set,\n   and only when old move is off (see below);\n4. every premise of every step holds, recomputed from the instance and the\n   state;\n5. every candidate with cost ≤ k is a child, or is covered by a step, and\n   every chain of coverings ends at a child or -- under old move -- at a\n   customer in Q(S), with no cycle;\n6. the children, recursively, with Q inherited by the reinsertion test.\n\nStep 5 is what the two historical bugs violate: the 2026-09-26 cross-rule\ncycle has every step's premise true and no chain ending anywhere, and the\nclose-count bug has a `better` step whose premise the checker recomputes as\nfalse. Both are rejected (`tests/test_search_certificate.py`).\n\n**Which steps are locally checkable.** The subset rule, Theorem 1 and Theorem\n2 are checkable from the instance and the state alone, step by step -- every\nquantity in their premises is a function of `(N, S, O(S), k, r, q)`. What is\n*not* a property of one step is its termination: a Theorem 2 step \"r goes\nbecause q covers it\" is sound only if q is itself explored or covered by a\nchain that ends in something explored, and that is a property of the whole\nstep set at the node (the checker's step 5). Theorem 3 is checkable from the\n*path*, not the state: Q(S) is which siblings an ancestor searched, threaded\nthrough the reinsertion tests. The memo is checkable from the state alone only\nwithout old move: with it, a refuted state's subtree may rest on Q entries\ninherited from ancestors above the cited node, so the refutation belongs to\nthe path and a second path reaching the same set has no right to it. The\nemitter never combines the two, as the Python reference does not; the C does\n(Chu & Stuckey run both), and a certificate of the C's run in that\nconfiguration would need the checker to re-derive the cited subtree under the\nnew path's Q -- which is a replay, not a lookup. That is the precise form of\nthe kill criterion's answer, and it concerns Theorem 3 with the memo, not\nTheorem 2.\n\n**What the checker trusts.** The three theorems themselves, in the free-move\ncost model this search uses (Theorem 2's hypotheses as re-derived in\n`reports/better_move_bug.md` §7 and checked there by brute force over 124 M\napplications), and the cost cut. It trusts neither the search nor the emitter:\na certificate that checks is a refutation whether or not the program that\nproduced it was correct. This is the same division as a DRAT proof, which\ntrusts resolution and not the solver.\n\n**The emitter** is a copy of `decide(native=False)`'s search with the rule\nwitnesses recorded, extended by a Python port of the C's `better_move` (today's\nrule: subset rule first over the remaining customers, Theorem 2 last over its\nsurvivors, corrected close count) so that Theorem 2 steps can be certified at\nall -- the Python reference never had the rule. Two measured reverts,\n`old_close_count` and `old_rule_order`, reproduce the bugs for the tests. The\nemitter agrees with `decide(native=False)` in status and node count on every\nconfiguration it shares, and with the C under `csearch` (`better_move=True`,\n`better_move_dominators=0`) -- `tests/test_search_certificate.py`. It certifies\nthe search under the rules **as Chu & Stuckey publish them**, i.e.\n`decide(..., repaired_rules=False)`; the repaired rules became the default on\n2026-10-01 (loop0007 item 03) and the emitter does not model them yet.\n\nRun:\n\n    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage run --max-customers 20 --workers 16\n    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage run --max-customers 40 --min-customers 21 --workers 16 --deadline 60\n    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage tables\n    python -m learning.search_certificate --stage one --instance <name> --k <k>   # emit, check, print sizes\n\nWrites `learning/data/ensemble/search_certificate.csv` (one row per instance ×\nconfiguration: status, nodes, steps by rule, bytes raw and gzipped, emit and\ncheck seconds, the checker's verdict, the DRAT verdict of §17 and the lattice\noracle's) and `reports/search_certificate_tables.md`. Nothing is written to\n`solutions/`; no solver default is touched; nothing here decides `k` for\nanything.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport gzip\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport multiprocessing\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n\nDATA_DIR = Path(\"learning/data/ensemble\")\nDEFAULT_CSV = DATA_DIR / \"search_certificate.csv\"\nDEFAULT_OUT = Path(\"reports/search_certificate_tables.md\")\nPROOFS_CSV = Path(\"learning/data/proofs.csv\")\nDEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR = Path(\"benchmarks/instances\")\nDEFAULT_SOLUTIONS_DIR = Path(\"solutions\")\n\nCONFIGS = (\"default\", \"csearch\", \"memo\")\nRULES = (\"definite\", \"subset\", \"better\")\nSIZE_BANDS = ((0, 10), (11, 15), (16, 20), (21, 25), (26, 30), (31, 35), (36, 40),\n              (41, 200))\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# instance digest and masks -- the checker's own, sharing nothing with the search\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef matrix_sha256(instance: MOSPInstance) -> str:\n    matrix = np.asarray(instance.matrix, dtype=np.int8)\n    return hashlib.sha256(matrix.tobytes() + bytes(matrix.shape)).hexdigest()\n\n\ndef neighbourhoods(instance: MOSPInstance) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Self-inclusive neighbourhoods N[c] as bitmasks, from the matrix alone.\"\"\"\n    matrix = np.asarray(instance.matrix)\n    n, m = matrix.shape\n    masks = [0] * n\n    for p in range(m):\n        holders = np.flatnonzero(matrix[:, p])\n        mask = 0\n        for c in holders:\n            mask |= 1 << int(c)\n        for c in holders:\n            masks[int(c)] |= mask\n    return masks\n\n\ndef _bits(mask: int):\n    while mask:\n        low = mask & -mask\n        yield low.bit_length() - 1\n        mask ^= low\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the certificate\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Node:\n    id: int\n    parent: int | None\n    move: int | None\n    free: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n    kind: str = \"refuted\"          # refuted | memo | sat | aborted\n    memo_of: int | None = None\n    steps: list[list[int | str]] = field(default_factory=list)\n    children: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def to_json(self) -> dict:\n        out = {\"id\": self.id, \"parent\": self.parent, \"move\": self.move}\n        if self.free:\n            out[\"free\"] = self.free\n        if self.kind != \"refuted\":\n            out[\"kind\"] = self.kind\n        if self.memo_of is not None:\n            out[\"memo_of\"] = self.memo_of\n        if self.steps:\n            out[\"steps\"] = self.steps\n        if self.children:\n            out[\"children\"] = self.children\n        return out\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def from_json(data: dict) -> \"Node\":\n        return Node(id=int(data[\"id\"]), parent=data.get(\"parent\"), move=data.get(\"move\"),\n                    free=list(data.get(\"free\", [])), kind=data.get(\"kind\", \"refuted\"),\n                    memo_of=data.get(\"memo_of\"), steps=list(data.get(\"steps\", [])),\n                    children=list(data.get(\"children\", [])))\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Certificate:\n    instance_name: str\n    n_customers: int\n    n_patterns: int\n    matrix_sha256: str\n    k: int\n    config: dict\n    status: str                    # unsat | sat | unknown\n    nodes: list[Node]\n    branches: int                  # the search's node count (branch decisions)\n    order: list[int] | None = None\n    emit_seconds: float = 0.0\n\n    def to_json(self) -> dict:\n        return {\"format\": \"mosp-search-certificate-1\",\n                \"instance_name\": self.instance_name, \"n_customers\": self.n_customers,\n                \"n_patterns\": self.n_patterns, \"matrix_sha256\": self.matrix_sha256,\n                \"k\": self.k, \"config\": self.config, \"status\": self.status,\n                \"branches\": self.branches, \"order\": self.order,\n                \"nodes\": [node.to_json() for node in self.nodes]}\n\n    def dumps(self) -> bytes:\n        return json.dumps(self.to_json(), separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode()\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def loads(data: bytes | str) -> \"Certificate\":\n        obj = json.loads(data)\n        if obj.get(\"format\") != \"mosp-search-certificate-1\":\n            raise ValueError(\"not a search certificate\")\n        return Certificate(instance_name=obj[\"instance_name\"], n_customers=obj[\"n_customers\"],\n                           n_patterns=obj[\"n_patterns\"], matrix_sha256=obj[\"matrix_sha256\"],\n                           k=int(obj[\"k\"]), config=dict(obj[\"config\"]), status=obj[\"status\"],\n                           nodes=[Node.from_json(d) for d in obj[\"nodes\"]],\n                           branches=int(obj[\"branches\"]), order=obj.get(\"order\"))\n\n    def step_counts(self) -> dict[str, int]:\n        counts = {rule: 0 for rule in RULES}\n        for node in self.nodes:\n            for step in node.steps:\n                counts[str(step[0])] += 1\n        counts[\"memo\"] = sum(1 for node in self.nodes if node.kind == \"memo\")\n        counts[\"free\"] = sum(len(node.free) for node in self.nodes)\n        return counts\n\n    def sizes(self) -> dict[str, int]:\n        raw = self.dumps()\n        return {\"nodes\": len(self.nodes), \"steps\": sum(len(n.steps) for n in self.nodes),\n                \"bytes\": len(raw), \"gz_bytes\": len(gzip.compress(raw, 9))}\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the emitter: decide(native=False) with witnesses, plus the C's better move\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef emit(\n    instance: MOSPInstance,\n    k: int,\n    *,\n    subset_rule: bool = True,\n    definite_move: bool = True,\n    old_move: bool = True,\n    memo: bool = False,\n    better_move: bool = False,\n    better_move_dominators: int = 0,\n    old_close_count: bool = False,\n    old_rule_order: bool = False,\n    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n    deadline: float | None = None,\n) -> Certificate:\n    \"\"\"Search \"MOSP(instance) ≤ k?\" as `decide(native=False)` does, recording the tree.\n\n    `memo` with `old_move` is refused, as the Python reference refuses it. With\n    `better_move` the filter follows today's C: definite move, subset rule over\n    the remaining customers, Theorem 2 over the subset survivors with only the\n    first `better_move_dominators` (0: all) as dominators. `old_close_count`\n    and `old_rule_order` are the two 2026-09-26 bugs, for the tests.\n    \"\"\"\n    if old_move and memo:\n        raise ValueError(\"memo cannot be combined with old_move in a certificate: \"\n                         \"a refutation under old move belongs to its path\")\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    masks = neighbourhoods(instance)\n    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if masks[c]]\n    full = 0\n    for c in active:\n        full |= 1 << c\n    config = {\"subset_rule\": subset_rule, \"definite_move\": definite_move, \"old_move\": old_move,\n              \"memo\": memo, \"better_move\": better_move,\n              \"better_move_dominators\": better_move_dominators,\n              \"old_close_count\": old_close_count, \"old_rule_order\": old_rule_order}\n\n    nodes: list[Node] = []\n    path: list[int] = []\n    refuted: dict[int, int] = {}\n    state = {\"branches\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n\n    def new_node(parent: int | None, move: int | None) -> Node:\n        node = Node(id=len(nodes), parent=parent, move=move)\n        nodes.append(node)\n        return node\n\n    if not active:\n        root = new_node(None, None)\n        root.kind = \"sat\"\n        return Certificate(instance.name, instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns,\n                           matrix_sha256(instance), k, config, \"sat\", nodes, 0, [],\n                           time.monotonic() - started)\n\n    def search(node: Node, closed: int, opened: int, seen: int) -> bool:\n        mark = len(path)\n        free = 0\n        for c in _bits(full & ~closed):\n            if masks[c] & ~opened == 0:\n                free |= 1 << c\n        for c in _bits(free):\n            path.append(c)\n        node.free = list(_bits(free))\n        closed |= free\n        if closed == full:\n            node.kind = \"sat\"\n            return True\n        if memo and closed in refuted:\n            node.kind = \"memo\"\n            node.memo_of = refuted[closed]\n            del path[mark:]\n            return False\n\n        remaining = full & ~closed\n        seen &= remaining\n        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n        opens = {c: masks[c] & ~opened for c in _bits(remaining)}\n        playable = [(((opened | masks[c]) & ~closed).bit_count(), c) for c in _bits(candidates)]\n        playable = [(cost, c) for cost, c in playable if cost <= k]\n\n        if playable:\n            playable = _filter(playable, opens, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n        playable.sort()\n\n        for _, c in playable:\n            state[\"branches\"] += 1\n            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"branches\"] > max_nodes:\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n            if (deadline is not None and state[\"branches\"] % 256 == 0\n                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n                node.kind = \"aborted\"\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            here = len(path)\n            path.append(c)\n            inherited = 0\n            if old_move and seen:\n                for q in _bits(seen):\n                    cost = ((opened | masks[q] | masks[c]) & ~(closed | (1 << q))).bit_count()\n                    if cost <= k:\n                        inherited |= 1 << q\n            child = new_node(node.id, c)\n            node.children.append(child.id)\n            if search(child, closed | (1 << c), opened | masks[c], inherited):\n                return True\n            del path[here:]\n            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n                node.kind = \"aborted\"\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            seen |= 1 << c\n\n        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"]:\n            refuted[closed] = node.id\n        del path[mark:]\n        return False\n\n    root = new_node(None, None)\n    found = search(root, 0, 0, 0)\n    if found:\n        status, order = \"sat\", list(path)\n    elif state[\"aborted\"]:\n        status, order = \"unknown\", None\n    else:\n        status, order = \"unsat\", None\n    return Certificate(instance.name, instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns,\n                       matrix_sha256(instance), k, config, status, nodes, state[\"branches\"],\n                       order, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n\ndef _filter(playable, opens, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full):\n    \"\"\"The dominance filter with witnesses, in `decide`'s / the C's order.\"\"\"\n    if config[\"definite_move\"]:\n        for cost, q in playable:\n            own = opens[q]\n            if sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0) >= own.bit_count():\n                node.steps.append([\"definite\", q])\n                return [(cost, q)]\n    if not config[\"better_move\"]:\n        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n        return playable\n    if config[\"old_rule_order\"]:\n        playable = _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n    else:\n        if config[\"subset_rule\"]:\n            playable = _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude=0)\n        playable = _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full)\n    return playable\n\n\ndef _subset_pass(playable, opens, node, exclude):\n    kept, steps = [], []\n    for cost, c in playable:\n        own = opens[c]\n        dominator = None\n        for d, other in opens.items():\n            if d == c or (exclude >> d) & 1:\n                continue\n            if other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < c):\n                dominator = d\n                break\n        if dominator is None:\n            kept.append((cost, c))\n        else:\n            steps.append([\"subset\", c, dominator])\n    if not kept:\n        return playable\n    node.steps.extend(steps)\n    return kept\n\n\ndef _better_pass(playable, masks, closed, opened, k, node, config, full):\n    \"\"\"Theorem 2 as the C's `better_move_pass`: an earlier candidate may cover a later one.\"\"\"\n    if len(playable) <= 1:\n        return playable\n    limit = config[\"better_move_dominators\"]\n    limit = len(playable) if limit <= 0 or limit > len(playable) else limit\n    survivors, steps = [], []\n    for ri, (cost_r, r) in enumerate(playable):\n        cover = None\n        for qi in range(min(limit, ri)):\n            q = playable[qi][1]\n            if q == r:\n                continue\n            if _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, config[\"old_close_count\"], full):\n                cover = q\n                break\n        if cover is None:\n            survivors.append((cost_r, r))\n        else:\n            steps.append([\"better\", r, cover])\n    node.steps.extend(steps)\n    return survivors\n\n\ndef _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, old_close_count=False, full=None) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"close(q, S∪{r}) ≥ open(q, S∪{r}) with S++[r,q] playable in the paper's measure.\"\"\"\n    if full is None:\n        full = 0\n        for c, mask in enumerate(masks):\n            if mask:\n                full |= 1 << c\n    closed_r = closed | (1 << r)\n    opened_r = opened | masks[r]\n    if ((opened_r | masks[q]) & ~closed_r).bit_count() > k:\n        return False\n    own = masks[q] & ~opened_r\n    opened_by = own.bit_count()\n    closed_by = 0\n    for d in _bits(full & ~closed_r):\n        left = masks[d] & ~opened_r\n        if (left or old_close_count) and left & ~own == 0:\n            closed_by += 1\n    return closed_by >= opened_by\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the checker\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass CheckResult:\n    ok: bool\n    reason: str\n    nodes: int\n    steps: int\n    seconds: float\n\n\nclass Rejected(Exception):\n    pass\n\n\ndef check(instance: MOSPInstance, cert: Certificate) -> CheckResult:\n    \"\"\"Verify that `cert` is a refutation of \"MOSP(instance) ≤ k\" (see the module docstring).\"\"\"\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    counted = {\"nodes\": 0, \"steps\": 0}\n    try:\n        _check(instance, cert, counted)\n    except Rejected as why:\n        return CheckResult(False, str(why), counted[\"nodes\"], counted[\"steps\"],\n                           time.monotonic() - started)\n    return CheckResult(True, \"refutation verified\", counted[\"nodes\"], counted[\"steps\"],\n                       time.monotonic() - started)\n\n\ndef _check(instance: MOSPInstance, cert: Certificate, counted: dict) -> None:\n    if cert.status != \"unsat\":\n        raise Rejected(f\"certificate status is {cert.status!r}, not a refutation\")\n    if cert.matrix_sha256 != matrix_sha256(instance):\n        raise Rejected(\"matrix digest does not match the instance\")\n    if (cert.n_customers, cert.n_patterns) != (instance.n_customers, instance.n_patterns):\n        raise Rejected(\"instance dimensions do not match\")\n    config = cert.config\n    old_move = bool(config.get(\"old_move\", False))\n    memo_allowed = bool(config.get(\"memo\", False)) and not old_move\n    if config.get(\"memo\") and old_move:\n        raise Rejected(\"memo references are not checkable under old move: \"\n                       \"a refuted state's subtree may rest on Q inherited along its path\")\n    k = cert.k\n    masks = neighbourhoods(instance)\n    full = 0\n    for c, mask in enumerate(masks):\n        if mask:\n            full |= 1 << c\n    nodes = {node.id: node for node in cert.nodes}\n    if len(nodes) != len(cert.nodes):\n        raise Rejected(\"duplicate node ids\")\n    if not cert.nodes or cert.nodes[0].parent is not None:\n        raise Rejected(\"no root\")\n    refuted_states: dict[int, int] = {}    # closed set -> node id, completed refutations\n    completed: set[int] = set()\n\n    def visit(node: Node, closed: int, opened: int, seen: int) -> None:\n        counted[\"nodes\"] += 1\n        # 1. free moves: exactly the remaining customers whose N is opened\n        computed_free = 0\n        for c in _bits(full & ~closed):\n            if masks[c] & ~opened == 0:\n                computed_free |= 1 << c\n        listed = 0\n        for c in node.free:\n            listed |= 1 << int(c)\n        if listed != computed_free:\n            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: free moves {node.free} are not the customers \"\n                           f\"whose neighbourhood is opened\")\n        closed |= computed_free\n        # 2. not a solution\n        if closed == full:\n            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: every customer is closed -- the tree holds a solution\")\n        if node.kind in (\"sat\", \"aborted\"):\n            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: kind {node.kind!r} inside a refutation\")\n        # 3. memo\n        if node.kind == \"memo\":\n            if not memo_allowed:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo reference without memo enabled\")\n            cited = node.memo_of\n            if cited is None or cited not in completed:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo cites node {cited}, not a completed refutation\")\n            if refuted_states.get(closed) != cited:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: memo cites node {cited}, whose closed set differs\")\n            if node.children or node.steps:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: a memo node carries children or steps\")\n            return\n        if node.kind != \"refuted\":\n            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: unknown kind {node.kind!r}\")\n\n        remaining = full & ~closed\n        seen &= remaining\n        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n        opens = {c: masks[c] & ~opened for c in _bits(remaining)}\n        cost = {c: ((opened | masks[c]) & ~closed).bit_count() for c in _bits(candidates)}\n        playable = {c for c, v in cost.items() if v <= k}\n        children = []\n        for cid in node.children:\n            child = nodes.get(int(cid))\n            if child is None or child.parent != node.id or child.move is None:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: child id {cid} missing or mis-parented\")\n            children.append(int(child.move))\n        child_set = set(children)\n        if len(child_set) != len(children):\n            raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: repeated child\")\n        for c in children:\n            if c not in playable:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: child {c} is not a playable candidate\")\n\n        # 4. premises, building the covering relation\n        cover: dict[int, int] = {}\n        definite: int | None = None\n        for step in node.steps:\n            counted[\"steps\"] += 1\n            rule = step[0]\n            if rule == \"definite\":\n                q = int(step[1])\n                if q not in playable:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: definite move keeps {q}, not a playable candidate\")\n                own = opens[q]\n                closed_by = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n                if closed_by < own.bit_count():\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: definite move on {q}: close {closed_by} < open \"\n                                   f\"{own.bit_count()}\")\n                if definite is not None:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: two definite moves\")\n                definite = q\n                for r in playable:\n                    if r != q:\n                        cover.setdefault(r, q)\n            elif rule == \"subset\":\n                r, d = int(step[1]), int(step[2])\n                if r not in playable or d not in opens or d == r:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: subset step ({r}, {d}) names a non-candidate\")\n                other, own = opens[d], opens[r]\n                if not (other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < r)):\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: subset step ({r}, {d}): o({d}) is not a \"\n                                   f\"(tie-broken) subset of o({r})\")\n                if r in cover:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: {r} discarded twice\")\n                cover[r] = d\n            elif rule == \"better\":\n                r, q = int(step[1]), int(step[2])\n                if r not in playable or q not in playable or q == r:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: better step ({r}, {q}) names a non-candidate\")\n                if not _better_premise(masks, closed, opened, k, r, q, full=full):\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: better step ({r}, {q}): close(q, S∪{{r}}) < \"\n                                   f\"open(q, S∪{{r}}) or S++[r,q] not playable\")\n                if r in cover:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: {r} discarded twice\")\n                cover[r] = q\n            else:\n                raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: unknown rule {rule!r}\")\n\n        # 5. exhaustiveness: every playable candidate ends at a child or in Q(S)\n        for c in sorted(playable):\n            if c in child_set:\n                continue\n            here, trail = c, set()\n            while True:\n                if here in child_set:\n                    break\n                if old_move and (seen >> here) & 1:\n                    break                      # Theorem 3: searched at an ancestor\n                if here in trail:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: covering cycle through {sorted(trail)}\")\n                trail.add(here)\n                if here not in cover:\n                    raise Rejected(f\"node {node.id}: candidate {here} (cost {cost.get(here)}) is \"\n                                   f\"neither explored nor covered\")\n                here = cover[here]\n\n        # 6. children in order, Q inherited by the reinsertion test\n        for cid in node.children:\n            child = nodes[int(cid)]\n            c = int(child.move)\n            inherited = 0\n            if old_move and seen:\n                for q in _bits(seen):\n                    if ((opened | masks[q] | masks[c]) & ~(closed | (1 << q))).bit_count() <= k:\n                        inherited |= 1 << q\n            visit(child, closed | (1 << c), opened | masks[c], inherited)\n            seen |= 1 << c\n        completed.add(node.id)\n        if memo_allowed:\n            refuted_states[closed] = node.id\n\n    visit(cert.nodes[0], 0, 0, 0)\n    if counted[\"nodes\"] != len(cert.nodes):\n        raise Rejected(f\"{len(cert.nodes) - counted['nodes']} nodes are not reachable from the root\")\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# the study: the corpus at n ≤ 20 (and beyond) against DRAT and the lattice\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef config_kwargs(instance: MOSPInstance, config: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"The emitter's keyword arguments under a named configuration.\n\n    `default`: the Python reference (subset rule, Theorem 1, Theorem 3, no memo).\n    `csearch`: the same with Theorem 2 where `sparse_enough_for_better_move`\n    says so, every candidate a dominator -- the configuration that certified\n    the corpus. `memo`: the subset rule, Theorem 1 and the memo, no Theorem 3 --\n    the one configuration whose memo references the checker accepts.\n    \"\"\"\n    if config == \"default\":\n        return {}\n    if config == \"csearch\":\n        from satisfiability.customer_search import sparse_enough_for_better_move\n\n        return {\"better_move\": sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance),\n                \"better_move_dominators\": 0}\n    if config == \"memo\":\n        return {\"old_move\": False, \"memo\": True}\n    raise ValueError(f\"unknown configuration {config!r}; one of {CONFIGS}\")\n\n\ndef certify_refutation(instance: MOSPInstance, optimum: int, config: str,\n                       deadline_seconds: float | None = None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Emit at `optimum − 1`, check, and describe the certificate in one row.\"\"\"\n    kwargs = config_kwargs(instance, config)\n    deadline = None if deadline_seconds is None else time.monotonic() + deadline_seconds\n    cert = emit(instance, optimum - 1, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n    row = {\"config\": config, \"k\": optimum - 1, \"status\": cert.status, \"branches\": cert.branches,\n           \"better_move\": bool(kwargs.get(\"better_move\", False)),\n           \"emit_seconds\": round(cert.emit_seconds, 5)}\n    row.update({f\"steps_{rule}\": v for rule, v in cert.step_counts().items()})\n    row.update(cert.sizes())\n    if cert.status == \"unsat\":\n        result = check(instance, Certificate.loads(cert.dumps()))\n        row.update(check_ok=result.ok, check_reason=result.reason,\n                   check_seconds=round(result.seconds, 5))\n    else:\n        row.update(check_ok=None, check_reason=\"\", check_seconds=None)\n    return row\n\n\ndef _job(args) -> list[dict]:\n    matrix, name, source_file, collection, optimum, configs, deadline = args\n    instance = MOSPInstance(matrix=np.array(matrix, dtype=np.int8), n_customers=len(matrix),\n                            n_patterns=len(matrix[0]), name=name)\n    base = {\"instance_name\": name, \"source_file\": source_file, \"collection\": collection,\n            \"n\": instance.n_customers, \"m\": instance.n_patterns, \"optimum\": int(optimum)}\n    oracle = None\n    try:\n        from learning.differential import oracle_minima\n        minima = oracle_minima(instance)\n        if minima is not None:\n            oracle = int(minima[\"oracle_search\"])\n    except Exception:          # the oracle is a cross-check, never a blocker\n        oracle = None\n    rows = []\n    for config in configs:\n        row = dict(base)\n        row.update(certify_refutation(instance, int(optimum), config, deadline))\n        row[\"oracle_search\"] = oracle\n        rows.append(row)\n    return rows\n\n\ndef targets(min_customers: int, max_customers: int, instance_dir: Path = DEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR,\n            solutions_dir: Path = DEFAULT_SOLUTIONS_DIR, limit: int | None = None) -> list[tuple]:\n    from learning.node_counts import certified_targets\n\n    jobs = [job for job in certified_targets(instance_dir, solutions_dir, max_customers, None)\n            if len(job[0]) >= min_customers]\n    return jobs[:limit] if limit is not None else jobs\n\n\ndef run(jobs: list[tuple], configs: tuple[str, ...], workers: int, deadline: float | None,\n        table: Path = DEFAULT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    table.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    done: set[tuple[str, str, str]] = set()\n    if table.exists():\n        old = pd.read_csv(table)\n        done = set(zip(old.instance_name, old.source_file, old.config))\n    todo = [(m, name, src, coll, opt, tuple(c for c in configs if (name, src, c) not in done), deadline)\n            for m, name, src, coll, opt, _ in jobs]\n    todo = [job for job in todo if job[5]]\n    print(f\"{len(todo)} instances to certify under {configs} on {workers} workers\", flush=True)\n    header = not table.exists()\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    with multiprocessing.get_context(\"spawn\").Pool(workers) as pool:\n        with table.open(\"a\") as handle:\n            for i, rows in enumerate(pool.imap_unordered(_job, todo, chunksize=1), 1):\n                frame = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n                frame.to_csv(handle, header=header, index=False)\n                header = False\n                if i % 500 == 0:\n                    print(f\"  {i}/{len(todo)} in {time.monotonic() - started:.0f} s\", flush=True)\n    return pd.read_csv(table)\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# tables\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef _band(n: int) -> str:\n    for lo, hi in SIZE_BANDS:\n        if lo <= n <= hi:\n            return f\"{lo}–{hi}\" if hi < 200 else f\"{lo}+\"\n    return \"?\"\n\n\ndef _md(frame: pd.DataFrame, floatfmt: str = \".3g\") -> str:\n    return frame.to_markdown(index=False, floatfmt=floatfmt)\n\n\ndef join_verdicts(frame: pd.DataFrame, proofs_csv: Path = PROOFS_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"Beside each row, §17's DRAT verdict and the lattice oracle's agreement.\"\"\"\n    out = frame.copy()\n    if proofs_csv.exists():\n        proofs = pd.read_csv(proofs_csv)[[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\", \"verdict\", \"status\"]]\n        proofs = proofs.rename(columns={\"verdict\": \"drat_verdict\", \"status\": \"drat_status\"})\n        out = out.merge(proofs, on=[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\"], how=\"left\")\n    else:\n        out[\"drat_verdict\"] = None\n        out[\"drat_status\"] = None\n    out[\"oracle_agrees\"] = out.oracle_search.notna() & (out.oracle_search == out.optimum)\n    out[\"oracle_disagrees\"] = out.oracle_search.notna() & (out.oracle_search != out.optimum)\n    return out\n\n\ndef summarise(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> dict[str, pd.DataFrame]:\n    frame = join_verdicts(frame)\n    frame[\"band\"] = frame.n.map(_band)\n    frame[\"band_lo\"] = frame.n.map(lambda n: next(lo for lo, hi in SIZE_BANDS if lo <= n <= hi))\n    tables: dict[str, pd.DataFrame] = {}\n\n    # verdicts per configuration\n    rows = []\n    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n        unsat = part[part.status == \"unsat\"]\n        drat = part[part.drat_verdict == \"verified\"]\n        oracle = part[part.oracle_search.notna()]\n        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"instances\": len(part),\n                     \"refuted (unsat)\": len(unsat), \"unknown (deadline)\": int((part.status == \"unknown\").sum()),\n                     \"sat below optimum\": int((part.status == \"sat\").sum()),\n                     \"certificates verified\": int(unsat.check_ok.fillna(False).astype(bool).sum()),\n                     \"certificates rejected\": int((~unsat.check_ok.fillna(True).astype(bool)).sum()),\n                     \"DRAT verified (§17)\": len(drat),\n                     \"both certificate and DRAT\": int(((unsat.check_ok == True) & (unsat.drat_verdict == \"verified\")).sum()),\n                     \"certificate where DRAT censored\": int(((unsat.check_ok == True) & (unsat.drat_verdict != \"verified\")).sum()),\n                     \"lattice oracle available\": len(oracle),\n                     \"oracle agrees\": int(oracle.oracle_agrees.sum()),\n                     \"oracle disagrees\": int(oracle.oracle_disagrees.sum())})\n    tables[\"verdicts\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n    # size and time by band, per config\n    rows = []\n    for (config, lo, band), part in frame.groupby([\"config\", \"band_lo\", \"band\"]):\n        unsat = part[(part.status == \"unsat\")]\n        if unsat.empty:\n            continue\n        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"band\": band, \"instances\": len(unsat),\n                     \"nodes median\": unsat.nodes.median(), \"nodes p90\": unsat.nodes.quantile(0.9),\n                     \"nodes max\": unsat.nodes.max(),\n                     \"steps median\": unsat.steps.median(), \"steps max\": unsat.steps.max(),\n                     \"bytes median\": unsat.bytes.median(), \"bytes max\": unsat.bytes.max(),\n                     \"gz bytes median\": unsat.gz_bytes.median(), \"gz bytes max\": unsat.gz_bytes.max(),\n                     \"emit ms median\": 1000 * unsat.emit_seconds.median(),\n                     \"check ms median\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.median(),\n                     \"check ms p90\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.quantile(0.9),\n                     \"check ms max\": 1000 * unsat.check_seconds.max(),\n                     \"bytes / branch\": (unsat.bytes.sum() / max(1, unsat.branches.sum()))})\n    tables[\"by_band\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows).sort_values([\"config\", \"band\"], key=lambda s: s if s.name == \"config\" else s.map(lambda b: int(str(b).split(\"–\")[0].rstrip(\"+\"))))\n\n    # steps by rule per config\n    rows = []\n    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n        unsat = part[part.status == \"unsat\"]\n        total_steps = max(1, unsat.steps.sum())\n        row = {\"config\": config, \"refutations\": len(unsat), \"branches\": int(unsat.branches.sum()),\n               \"nodes\": int(unsat.nodes.sum()), \"steps\": int(unsat.steps.sum())}\n        for rule in (\"definite\", \"subset\", \"better\", \"memo\", \"free\"):\n            col = f\"steps_{rule}\"\n            row[rule] = int(unsat[col].sum()) if col in unsat else 0\n        row[\"zero-step refutations\"] = int((unsat.steps == 0).sum())\n        row[\"zero-branch refutations\"] = int((unsat.branches == 0).sum())\n        row[\"steps / branch\"] = unsat.steps.sum() / max(1, unsat.branches.sum())\n        rows.append(row)\n    tables[\"rules\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n    # check time against size: seconds per node, per step\n    rows = []\n    for config, part in frame.groupby(\"config\"):\n        unsat = part[(part.status == \"unsat\") & part.check_seconds.notna()]\n        if unsat.empty:\n            continue\n        rows.append({\"config\": config, \"refutations\": len(unsat),\n                     \"check s total\": unsat.check_seconds.sum(),\n                     \"emit s total\": unsat.emit_seconds.sum(),\n                     \"check / emit\": unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1e-9, unsat.emit_seconds.sum()),\n                     \"µs per node\": 1e6 * unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1, unsat.nodes.sum()),\n                     \"µs per step\": 1e6 * unsat.check_seconds.sum() / max(1, unsat.steps.sum()),\n                     \"bytes total\": int(unsat.bytes.sum()), \"gz bytes total\": int(unsat.gz_bytes.sum())})\n    tables[\"cost\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n    # against the DRAT proofs of §17: the same instances, size and check time\n    rows = []\n    proofs = pd.read_csv(PROOFS_CSV) if PROOFS_CSV.exists() else None\n    if proofs is not None:\n        cs = frame[(frame.config == \"csearch\") & (frame.status == \"unsat\") & (frame.check_ok == True)]\n        both = cs.merge(proofs[proofs.verdict == \"verified\"][\n            [\"instance_name\", \"source_file\", \"proof_gz_bytes\", \"proof_bytes\", \"proof_lemmas\",\n             \"check_seconds\", \"solve_seconds\", \"conflicts\"]].rename(\n                columns={\"check_seconds\": \"drat_check_seconds\", \"solve_seconds\": \"drat_solve_seconds\"}),\n            on=[\"instance_name\", \"source_file\"], how=\"inner\")\n        for (lo, band), part in both.groupby([\"band_lo\", \"band\"]):\n            rows.append({\"band\": band, \"instances (both proofs)\": len(part),\n                         \"certificate gz bytes median\": part.gz_bytes.median(),\n                         \"DRAT gz bytes median\": part.proof_gz_bytes.median(),\n                         \"gz ratio DRAT / certificate (median of ratios)\": (part.proof_gz_bytes / part.gz_bytes).median(),\n                         \"certificate check ms median\": 1000 * part.check_seconds.median(),\n                         \"DRAT check ms median\": 1000 * part.drat_check_seconds.median(),\n                         \"check ratio DRAT / certificate (median)\": (part.drat_check_seconds / part.check_seconds.clip(lower=1e-6)).median(),\n                         \"certificate gz total MB\": part.gz_bytes.sum() / 1e6,\n                         \"DRAT gz total MB\": part.proof_gz_bytes.sum() / 1e6,\n                         \"certificate check s total\": part.check_seconds.sum(),\n                         \"DRAT check s total\": part.drat_check_seconds.sum(),\n                         \"emit s total\": part.emit_seconds.sum(),\n                         \"DRAT solve s total\": part.drat_solve_seconds.sum()})\n    tables[\"against_drat\"] = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\n    # rejected or disagreeing rows, if any\n    bad = frame[((frame.status == \"unsat\") & (frame.check_ok == False))\n                | (frame.status == \"sat\") | frame.oracle_disagrees]\n    tables[\"flagged\"] = bad[[\"instance_name\", \"config\", \"n\", \"m\", \"optimum\", \"status\", \"check_ok\",\n                             \"check_reason\", \"oracle_search\", \"drat_verdict\"]].head(50)\n    return tables\n\n\ndef write_tables(frame: pd.DataFrame, out: Path = DEFAULT_OUT) -> None:\n    tables = summarise(frame)\n    parts = [\"# Search certificates: tables (loop0004 item 05)\", \"\",\n             f\"*Generated by `python -m learning.search_certificate --stage tables` from \"\n             f\"`{DEFAULT_CSV}`: {len(frame)} rows, {frame.instance_name.nunique()} instances at \"\n             f\"{frame.n.min()}–{frame.n.max()} customers.*\", \"\"]\n    titles = {\"verdicts\": \"Verdicts per configuration, against §17's DRAT proofs and the lattice oracle (n_active ≤ 15)\",\n              \"by_band\": \"Certificate size and check time by size band (refutations only)\",\n              \"rules\": \"Steps by rule\",\n              \"cost\": \"Check cost against emit cost and size\",\n              \"against_drat\": \"Against §17's DRAT proofs on the instances that have both (csearch configuration)\",\n              \"flagged\": \"Rejected certificates, satisfiable calls below the optimum, oracle disagreements\"}\n    for key, title in titles.items():\n        parts += [f\"## {title}\", \"\", _md(tables[key]) if not tables[key].empty else \"*(none)*\", \"\"]\n    out.write_text(\"\\n\".join(parts))\n    print(f\"wrote {out}\")\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# entry point\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split(\"\\n\\n\")[0])\n    parser.add_argument(\"--stage\", choices=(\"run\", \"tables\", \"one\"), default=\"tables\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--configs\", default=\",\".join(CONFIGS))\n    parser.add_argument(\"--min-customers\", type=int, default=0)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--max-customers\", type=int, default=20)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--workers\", type=int, default=16)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--deadline\", type=float, default=None, help=\"seconds per emit call\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=None)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--table\", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_CSV)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--out\", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_OUT)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--instance\", type=str, default=None)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--k\", type=int, default=None)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--save\", type=Path, default=None, help=\"write the certificate (gzip JSON)\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    configs = tuple(c for c in args.configs.split(\",\") if c)\n\n    if args.stage == \"run\":\n        jobs = targets(args.min_customers, args.max_customers, limit=args.limit)\n        frame = run(jobs, configs, args.workers, args.deadline, args.table)\n        write_tables(frame, args.out)\n    elif args.stage == \"tables\":\n        write_tables(pd.read_csv(args.table), args.out)\n    else:\n        from learning.dataset import enumerate_instances\n\n        found = None\n        for _, inst in enumerate_instances(Path(DEFAULT_INSTANCE_DIR)):\n            if inst.name == args.instance:\n                found = inst\n                break\n        if found is None:\n            raise SystemExit(f\"no instance named {args.instance!r}\")\n        for config in configs:\n            cert = emit(found, args.k, **config_kwargs(found, config))\n            result = check(found, cert) if cert.status == \"unsat\" else None\n            print(config, cert.status, \"branches\", cert.branches, cert.sizes(), cert.step_counts(),\n                  result)\n            if args.save is not None:\n                args.save.write_bytes(gzip.compress(cert.dumps()))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","numLines":991,"startLine":1,"totalLines":991}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018rqE5yfHZoUFjjAqzpPoN9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n2\t\n3\tAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\n4\tspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\n5\tstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\n6\tthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\n7\tbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n8\t\n9\tWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\n10\tsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\n11\tagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\n12\tThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\n13\topen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\n14\tslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\n15\ttheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n16\t\n17\t**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\n18\tthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n19\t`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\n20\tbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\n21\tcloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\n22\tmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n23\t\n24\t**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\n25\tcosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\n26\ttheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\n27\tcustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\n28\tthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\n29\twrong.\n30\t\n31\t**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\n32\tbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n33\t\n34\t- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n35\t  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n36\t  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n37\t- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n38\t  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n39\t  so **all** other branches go.\n40\t- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n41\t  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n42\t  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n43\t- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n44\t  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n45\t  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, and **worth it only on sparse instances** --\n46\t  see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`. Implemented in the C first; the Python\n47\t  port (2026-10-01) matches it node for node.\n48\t\n49\t**Two of the published theorems are false as stated** (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md`\n50\t§4.3.2, §4.3.4): the definite move can discard the last solution at a node, and\n51\tthe better move inherits the fault. `repaired_rules=True` applies the premises\n52\tproved sound in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, under which the whole search\n53\tis proved sound (`exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue`); it is the default since\n54\t2026-10-01 (loop0007 item 03), and `False` keeps the published rules for\n55\tcomparison.\n56\t\n57\t**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n58\t\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\n59\tPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\n60\tbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\n61\tan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\n62\tand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\n63\twould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\n64\tin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n65\t\"\"\"\n66\t\n67\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n68\t\n69\timport time\n70\tfrom collections.abc import Callable\n71\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n72\t\n73\tfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n74\tfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\n75\tfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n76\t    FAN_ORDERS,\n77\t    _neighbour_masks,\n78\t    fan_sort_key,\n79\t    product_order_from_customers,\n80\t)\n81\t\n82\t\n83\t@dataclass\n84\tclass Decision:\n85\t    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n86\t\n87\t    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n88\t    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n89\t    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n90\t    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n91\t    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n92\t    \"\"\"\n93\t\n94\t    status: str\n95\t    order: list[int] | None\n96\t    nodes: int\n97\t\n98\t\n99\t# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n100\t# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n101\t# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n102\t#\n103\t#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n104\t#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n105\t#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n106\t#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n107\t#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n108\t#\n109\t# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n110\t# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n111\t# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n112\t# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\n113\tBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n114\t\n115\t\n116\tdef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n117\t    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n118\t\n119\t    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n120\t    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n121\t    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n122\t    a \"-8-\").\n123\t    \"\"\"\n124\t    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n125\t        return False\n126\t    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n127\t\n128\t\n129\tdef decide(\n130\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n131\t    k: int,\n132\t    *,\n133\t    restrict: bool = False,\n134\t    subset_rule: bool = True,\n135\t    definite_move: bool = True,\n136\t    old_move: bool = True,\n137\t    memo: bool = True,\n138\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n139\t    deadline: float | None = None,\n140\t    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n141\t    native: bool = True,\n142\t    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n143\t    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n144\t    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n145\t    better_move: bool = False,\n146\t    better_move_dominators: int = 4,\n147\t    repaired_rules: bool = True,\n148\t    **kwargs: object,\n149\t) -> Decision:\n150\t    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n151\t\n152\t    Args:\n153\t        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n154\t        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n155\t            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n156\t            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n157\t        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n158\t            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n159\t            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n160\t        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n161\t            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n162\t            failure by whatever path reached it.\n163\t        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n164\t        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n165\t            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n166\t            budget actually has.\n167\t        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n168\t            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n169\t            refutation will take the machine down.\n170\t        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n171\t            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n172\t            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n173\t            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n174\t            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n175\t        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n176\t            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n177\t            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n178\t            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n179\t            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n180\t            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n181\t            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n182\t            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n183\t            writing.\n184\t        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n185\t            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n186\t            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n187\t            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n188\t            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n189\t            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n190\t            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n191\t            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n192\t            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n193\t            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n194\t        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n195\t            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n196\t            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n197\t            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n198\t            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n199\t            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n200\t            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n201\t            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n202\t            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n203\t        better_move: Theorem 2, run after the subset rule over its survivors,\n204\t            each candidate `r` dropped if an *earlier* survivor `q` (among the\n205\t            first `better_move_dominators` of them, every earlier one if 0)\n206\t            meets premises 3 and 4. A port of the C's `better_move_pass`, so\n207\t            that the reference covers every rule the C runs; before\n208\t            2026-10-01 the Python had no Theorem 2 and the flag was inert here.\n209\t        repaired_rules: the definite and better moves with the *repaired*\n210\t            premises proved sound in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`\n211\t            (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.6.1). The definite move fires on\n212\t            the first playable `q` passing `close ≥ open` *and* the matching\n213\t            test of `HasDefiniteMatching` -- some `open(q, S) − 1` of the\n214\t            customers `q` frees can be matched to distinct stacks among the\n215\t            ones each newly needs, equivalent to `IsHereditarilyDefinite` --\n216\t            and a candidate failing the matching is passed over, not fatal.\n217\t            The better move cites `q` for `r` only if, besides premises 3 and\n218\t            4, `q` passes the same matching test at `cl(S ∪ {r})`\n219\t            (`IsRepairedBetter`). The old tests stay in front as cheap\n220\t            prefilters, which the repair implies. Implemented in the C as\n221\t            well (item 02), node for node, and in the pathwidth solver (item\n222\t            03). **The default since 2026-10-01** (loop0007 item 03, the\n223\t            owner's decision); `False` is the published rules, kept for\n224\t            comparison and regression.\n225\t\n226\t    Returns:\n227\t        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n228\t        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n229\t    \"\"\"\n230\t    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n231\t        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n232\t\n233\t    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n234\t        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n235\t        answer = decide_native(\n236\t            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n237\t            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n238\t            better_move=better_move,\n239\t            better_move_dominators=better_move_dominators,\n240\t            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n241\t            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n242\t            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n243\t            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n244\t            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n245\t            repaired_rules=repaired_rules,\n246\t            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n247\t            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n248\t            fan_order=fan_order)\n249\t        if answer is not None:\n250\t            return answer\n251\t\n252\t    if old_move and memo:\n253\t        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n254\t        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n255\t        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n256\t        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n257\t        memo = False\n258\t\n259\t    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n260\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n261\t\n262\t    full = 0\n263\t    for customer in active:\n264\t        full |= 1 << customer\n265\t\n266\t    if not active:\n267\t        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n268\t\n269\t    path: list[int] = []\n270\t    refuted: set[int] = set()\n271\t    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n272\t\n273\t    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n274\t        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n275\t\n276\t        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n277\t        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n278\t        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n279\t        \"\"\"\n280\t        found = 0\n281\t        bits = full & ~closed\n282\t        while bits:\n283\t            bit = bits & -bits\n284\t            bits ^= bit\n285\t            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n286\t                found |= bit\n287\t        return found\n288\t\n289\t    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n290\t        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n291\t        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n292\t        mark = len(path)\n293\t\n294\t        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n295\t        if free:\n296\t            bits = free\n297\t            while bits:\n298\t                bit = bits & -bits\n299\t                bits ^= bit\n300\t                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n301\t            closed |= free\n302\t\n303\t        if closed == full:\n304\t            return True\n305\t        if memo and closed in refuted:\n306\t            del path[mark:]\n307\t            return False\n308\t\n309\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n310\t        seen &= remaining\n311\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n312\t        if restrict:\n313\t            narrowed = remaining & opened\n314\t            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n315\t            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n316\t            if narrowed:\n317\t                candidates = narrowed\n318\t\n319\t        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n320\t        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n321\t        bits = remaining\n322\t        while bits:\n323\t            bit = bits & -bits\n324\t            bits ^= bit\n325\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n326\t            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n327\t\n328\t        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n329\t        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n330\t        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n331\t        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n332\t        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n333\t        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n334\t        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n335\t        # stays sound.\n336\t        if expansion_prune and opens:\n337\t            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n338\t            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n339\t            floor = 0\n340\t            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n341\t                need = size - step + 1\n342\t                if need > floor:\n343\t                    floor = need\n344\t            if open_now + floor > k:\n345\t                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n346\t                    refuted.add(closed)\n347\t                del path[mark:]\n348\t                return False\n349\t\n350\t        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n351\t        bits = candidates\n352\t        while bits:\n353\t            bit = bits & -bits\n354\t            bits ^= bit\n355\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n356\t            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n357\t            if cost <= k:\n358\t                playable.append((cost, customer))\n359\t\n360\t        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move or better_move):\n361\t            playable = _apply_dominance(\n362\t                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move,\n363\t                better_move=better_move, dominators=better_move_dominators,\n364\t                repaired=repaired_rules, masks=masks, full=full,\n365\t                closed=closed, opened=opened, k=k)\n366\t\n367\t        if fan_order == \"index\":\n368\t            playable.sort()\n369\t        else:\n370\t            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n371\t        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n372\t            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n373\t        for _, customer in playable:\n374\t            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n375\t            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n376\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n377\t                del path[mark:]\n378\t                return False\n379\t            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n380\t                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n381\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n382\t                del path[mark:]\n383\t                return False\n384\t\n385\t            here = len(path)\n386\t            path.append(customer)\n387\t            inherited = 0\n388\t            if old_move and seen:\n389\t                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n390\t                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n391\t                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n392\t                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n393\t                bits = seen\n394\t                while bits:\n395\t                    bit = bits & -bits\n396\t                    bits ^= bit\n397\t                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n398\t                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n399\t                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n400\t                    if cost <= k:\n401\t                        inherited |= bit\n402\t            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n403\t                      inherited):\n404\t                return True\n405\t            del path[here:]\n406\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n407\t                del path[mark:]\n408\t                return False\n409\t            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n410\t            # instead would be repeating it.\n411\t            seen |= 1 << customer\n412\t\n413\t        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n414\t        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n415\t        # permanent wrong answer.\n416\t        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n417\t            refuted.add(closed)\n418\t        del path[mark:]\n419\t        return False\n420\t\n421\t    found = search(0, 0)\n422\t    if found:\n423\t        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n424\t    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n425\t        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n426\t    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n427\t\n428\t\n429\tdef _apply_dominance(\n430\t    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n431\t    opens: dict[int, int],\n432\t    subset_rule: bool,\n433\t    definite_move: bool,\n434\t    *,\n435\t    better_move: bool = False,\n436\t    dominators: int = 4,\n437\t    repaired: bool = False,\n438\t    masks: list[int] | None = None,\n439\t    full: int = 0,\n440\t    closed: int = 0,\n441\t    opened: int = 0,\n442\t    k: int = 0,\n443\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n444\t    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the dominance relations, in the order\n445\t    definite move, subset rule, better move, each citing only candidates still\n446\t    standing (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.4.1).\n447\t\n448\t    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n449\t    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n450\t    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n451\t    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n452\t\n453\t    `playable` arrives in customer index order, which is the order the better\n454\t    move's \"earlier\" and its dominator limit refer to, as in the C.\n455\t    `better_move` needs `masks`, `full`, `closed`, `opened` and `k`.\n456\t    \"\"\"\n457\t    if definite_move:\n458\t        for cost, q in playable:\n459\t            own = opens[q]\n460\t            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n461\t            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n462\t            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n463\t                # close(q, S) counts q itself; the customers q frees are the rest.\n464\t                if repaired and not _has_definite_matching(\n465\t                        [other for d, other in opens.items()\n466\t                         if d != q and other & ~own == 0],\n467\t                        opened_by_q - 1):\n468\t                    # Chu & Stuckey's premise holds but the hereditary one does\n469\t                    # not: a solution may avoid q (Counterexample 4.5), so q\n470\t                    # may not stand for the others. A later q may still.\n471\t                    continue\n472\t                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n473\t                # branch can go.\n474\t                return [(cost, q)]\n475\t\n476\t    kept = _subset_survivors(playable, opens) if subset_rule else playable\n477\t    if better_move and len(kept) > 1:\n478\t        kept = _better_move_pass(kept, masks, full, closed, opened, k,\n479\t                                 dominators, repaired)\n480\t    return kept\n481\t\n482\t\n483\tdef _subset_survivors(\n484\t    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n485\t    opens: dict[int, int],\n486\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n487\t    \"\"\"The subset rule with its index tie-break, dominators from every remaining customer.\"\"\"\n488\t    kept = []\n489\t    for cost, q in playable:\n490\t        own = opens[q]\n491\t        dominated = any(\n492\t            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n493\t            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n494\t        )\n495\t        if not dominated:\n496\t            kept.append((cost, q))\n497\t    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n498\t    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n499\t    return kept or playable\n500\t\n501\t\n502\tdef _better_move_pass(\n503\t    kept: list[tuple[int, int]],\n504\t    masks: list[int],\n505\t    full: int,\n506\t    closed: int,\n507\t    opened: int,\n508\t    k: int,\n509\t    dominators: int,\n510\t    repaired: bool,\n511\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n512\t    \"\"\"Theorem 2 over the subset survivors, the C's `better_move_pass`.\n513\t\n514\t    `r` goes if an earlier survivor `q`, among the first `dominators` (all if\n515\t    0), has premise 3, `|(O(S ∪ {r}) ∪ N[q]) − (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k`, and premise 4,\n516\t    `open' ≤ close'` with `close'` counting the `d ∉ S ∪ {r}` with\n517\t    `∅ ≠ N[d] − O(S ∪ {r}) ⊆ N[q] − O(S ∪ {r})`: Theorem 1's premise for `q`\n518\t    at the child `cl(S ∪ {r})` (Lemma 4.12). With `repaired`, also the\n519\t    matching test at that child, which makes the premise `IsRepairedBetter`.\n520\t    Only an earlier candidate may cite, so the first always survives.\n521\t    \"\"\"\n522\t    count = len(kept)\n523\t    limit = dominators if 0 < dominators < count else count\n524\t    survivors = []\n525\t    for ri, item in enumerate(kept):\n526\t        r = item[1]\n527\t        closed_r = closed | (1 << r)\n528\t        opened_r = opened | masks[r]\n529\t        remaining_r = full & ~closed_r\n530\t        pruned = False\n531\t        for qi in range(min(limit, ri)):\n532\t            q = kept[qi][1]\n533\t            if ((opened_r | masks[q]) & ~closed_r).bit_count() > k:       # premise 3\n534\t                continue\n535\t            own = masks[q] & ~opened_r\n536\t            opened_by = own.bit_count()\n537\t            freed = []\n538\t            closed_by = 0\n539\t            bits = remaining_r\n540\t            while bits:\n541\t                bit = bits & -bits\n542\t                bits ^= bit\n543\t                d = bit.bit_length() - 1\n544\t                left = masks[d] & ~opened_r\n545\t                # Customers r finishes alone are free in the child and are not\n546\t                # stacks q closes (Bug A, reports/better_move_bug.md §7).\n547\t                if left and left & ~own == 0:\n548\t                    closed_by += 1\n549\t                    if d != q:\n550\t                        freed.append(left)\n551\t            if closed_by < opened_by:                                      # premise 4\n552\t                continue\n553\t            if repaired and not _has_definite_matching(freed, opened_by - 1):\n554\t                continue\n555\t            pruned = True\n556\t            break\n557\t        if not pruned:\n558\t            survivors.append(item)\n559\t    return survivors\n560\t\n561\t\n562\tdef _has_definite_matching(freed: list[int], need: int) -> bool:\n563\t    \"\"\"Whether `need` of the sets in `freed` can be matched to distinct members.\n564\t\n565\t    `freed` holds `o(d, S)` for each customer `d ≠ q` that closing `q` frees, as\n566\t    stack bitmasks; `need` is `open(q, S) − 1`. By Theorem 4.8\n567\t    (`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`) the maximum matching\n568\t    reaching `need` is exactly `q` being hereditarily definite at `S`. Kuhn's\n569\t    augmenting paths, stopping as soon as `need` edges are matched; the sets\n570\t    are subsets of `o(q, S)`, so the stack side is small.\n571\t    \"\"\"\n572\t    if need <= 0:\n573\t        return True\n574\t    if len(freed) < need:\n575\t        return False\n576\t    owner: dict[int, int] = {}          # stack -> index into freed\n577\t\n578\t    def augment(i: int, visited: set[int]) -> bool:\n579\t        bits = freed[i]\n580\t        while bits:\n581\t            bit = bits & -bits\n582\t            bits ^= bit\n583\t            if bit in visited:\n584\t                continue\n585\t            visited.add(bit)\n586\t            if bit not in owner or augment(owner[bit], visited):\n587\t                owner[bit] = i\n588\t                return True\n589\t        return False\n590\t\n591\t    matched = 0\n592\t    for i in range(len(freed)):\n593\t        if augment(i, set()):\n594\t            matched += 1\n595\t            if matched >= need:\n596\t                return True\n597\t    return False\n598\t\n599\t\n600\t@dataclass\n601\tclass Solution:\n602\t    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n603\t\n604\t    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n605\t    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n606\t    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n607\t    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n608\t    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n609\t\n610\t    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n611\t    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n612\t    \"\"\"\n613\t\n614\t    value: int\n615\t    order: list[int]\n616\t    proof: str\n617\t    nodes: int\n618\t    seconds: float\n619\t\n620\t    @property\n621\t    def proved(self) -> bool:\n622\t        return bool(self.proof)\n623\t\n624\t\n625\tdef solve(\n626\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n627\t    *,\n628\t    upper: int | None = None,\n629\t    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n630\t    lower: int = 0,\n631\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n632\t    time_budget: float | None = None,\n633\t    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n634\t    **kwargs: object,\n635\t) -> Solution:\n636\t    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n637\t\n638\t    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n639\t    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n640\t    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n641\t    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n642\t    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n643\t\n644\t    Args:\n645\t        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n646\t        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n647\t            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n648\t        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n649\t            descent with `proved` false.\n650\t        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n651\t            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n652\t            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n653\t            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n654\t            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n655\t            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n656\t            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n657\t        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n658\t            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n659\t            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n660\t            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n661\t            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n662\t\n663\t    Returns:\n664\t        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n665\t        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n666\t    \"\"\"\n667\t    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n668\t\n669\t    started = time.monotonic()\n670\t    nodes = 0\n671\t\n672\t    if upper is None:\n673\t        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n674\t        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n675\t    else:\n676\t        value = upper\n677\t        order = []\n678\t\n679\t    if value <= lower:\n680\t        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n681\t\n682\t    k = value - 1\n683\t    while k >= lower:\n684\t        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n685\t        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n686\t        nodes += answer.nodes\n687\t\n688\t        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n689\t            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n690\t            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n691\t            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n692\t            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n693\t            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n694\t            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n695\t            # project has had to retract before.\n696\t            if value == k + 1:\n697\t                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n698\t                                time.monotonic() - started)\n699\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n700\t        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n701\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n702\t\n703\t        found = answer.order\n704\t        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n705\t            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n706\t        if achieved < value:\n707\t            value, order = achieved, found\n708\t            if on_improve is not None:\n709\t                on_improve(value, order)\n710\t        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n711\t\n712\t    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n713\t    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n714\t    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n715\t\n716\t\n717\tdef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n718\t    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n719\t    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n720\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n721\t    return sorted(\n722\t        active,\n723\t        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n724\t    )\n725\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be74ee69-bdd1-4ca9-adfe-51cb483bfc8a","uuid":"c3f1d597-da69-478b-af10-905bef5413c1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T04:33:30.091Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/satisfiability/customer_search.py","content":"\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n\nAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\nspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\nstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\nthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\nbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n\nWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\nsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\nagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\nThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\nopen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\nslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\ntheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n\n**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\nthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\nbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\ncloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\nmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n\n**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\ncosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\ntheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\ncustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\nthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\nwrong.\n\n**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\nbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n\n- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n  so **all** other branches go.\n- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, and **worth it only on sparse instances** --\n  see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`. Implemented in the C first; the Python\n  port (2026-10-01) matches it node for node.\n\n**Two of the published theorems are false as stated** (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md`\n§4.3.2, §4.3.4): the definite move can discard the last solution at a node, and\nthe better move inherits the fault. `repaired_rules=True` applies the premises\nproved sound in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, under which the whole search\nis proved sound (`exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue`); it is the default since\n2026-10-01 (loop0007 item 03), and `False` keeps the published rules for\ncomparison.\n\n**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\nPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\nbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\nan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\nand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\nwould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\nin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport time\nfrom collections.abc import Callable\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\nfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\nfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n    FAN_ORDERS,\n    _neighbour_masks,\n    fan_sort_key,\n    product_order_from_customers,\n)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Decision:\n    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n\n    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    status: str\n    order: list[int] | None\n    nodes: int\n\n\n# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n#\n#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n#\n# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\nBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n\n\ndef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n\n    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n    a \"-8-\").\n    \"\"\"\n    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n        return False\n    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n\n\ndef decide(\n    instance: MOSPInstance,\n    k: int,\n    *,\n    restrict: bool = False,\n    subset_rule: bool = True,\n    definite_move: bool = True,\n    old_move: bool = True,\n    memo: bool = True,\n    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n    deadline: float | None = None,\n    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n    native: bool = True,\n    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n    better_move: bool = False,\n    better_move_dominators: int = 4,\n    repaired_rules: bool = True,\n    **kwargs: object,\n) -> Decision:\n    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n\n    Args:\n        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n            failure by whatever path reached it.\n        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n            budget actually has.\n        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n            refutation will take the machine down.\n        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n            writing.\n        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n        better_move: Theorem 2, run after the subset rule over its survivors,\n            each candidate `r` dropped if an *earlier* survivor `q` (among the\n            first `better_move_dominators` of them, every earlier one if 0)\n            meets premises 3 and 4. A port of the C's `better_move_pass`, so\n            that the reference covers every rule the C runs; before\n            2026-10-01 the Python had no Theorem 2 and the flag was inert here.\n        repaired_rules: the definite and better moves with the *repaired*\n            premises proved sound in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`\n            (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.6.1). The definite move fires on\n            the first playable `q` passing `close ≥ open` *and* the matching\n            test of `HasDefiniteMatching` -- some `open(q, S) − 1` of the\n            customers `q` frees can be matched to distinct stacks among the\n            ones each newly needs, equivalent to `IsHereditarilyDefinite` --\n            and a candidate failing the matching is passed over, not fatal.\n            The better move cites `q` for `r` only if, besides premises 3 and\n            4, `q` passes the same matching test at `cl(S ∪ {r})`\n            (`IsRepairedBetter`). The old tests stay in front as cheap\n            prefilters, which the repair implies. Implemented in the C as\n            well (item 02), node for node, and in the pathwidth solver (item\n            03). **The default since 2026-10-01** (loop0007 item 03, the\n            owner's decision); `False` is the published rules, kept for\n            comparison and regression.\n\n    Returns:\n        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n    \"\"\"\n    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n\n    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n        answer = decide_native(\n            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n            better_move=better_move,\n            better_move_dominators=better_move_dominators,\n            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n            repaired_rules=repaired_rules,\n            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n            fan_order=fan_order)\n        if answer is not None:\n            return answer\n\n    if old_move and memo:\n        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n        memo = False\n\n    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n\n    full = 0\n    for customer in active:\n        full |= 1 << customer\n\n    if not active:\n        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n\n    path: list[int] = []\n    refuted: set[int] = set()\n    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n\n    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n\n        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n        \"\"\"\n        found = 0\n        bits = full & ~closed\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n                found |= bit\n        return found\n\n    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n        mark = len(path)\n\n        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n        if free:\n            bits = free\n            while bits:\n                bit = bits & -bits\n                bits ^= bit\n                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n            closed |= free\n\n        if closed == full:\n            return True\n        if memo and closed in refuted:\n            del path[mark:]\n            return False\n\n        remaining = full & ~closed\n        seen &= remaining\n        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n        if restrict:\n            narrowed = remaining & opened\n            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n            if narrowed:\n                candidates = narrowed\n\n        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n        bits = remaining\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n\n        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n        # stays sound.\n        if expansion_prune and opens:\n            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n            floor = 0\n            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n                need = size - step + 1\n                if need > floor:\n                    floor = need\n            if open_now + floor > k:\n                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n                    refuted.add(closed)\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n\n        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n        bits = candidates\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n            if cost <= k:\n                playable.append((cost, customer))\n\n        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move or better_move):\n            playable = _apply_dominance(\n                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move,\n                better_move=better_move, dominators=better_move_dominators,\n                repaired=repaired_rules, masks=masks, full=full,\n                closed=closed, opened=opened, k=k)\n\n        if fan_order == \"index\":\n            playable.sort()\n        else:\n            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n        for _, customer in playable:\n            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n\n            here = len(path)\n            path.append(customer)\n            inherited = 0\n            if old_move and seen:\n                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n                bits = seen\n                while bits:\n                    bit = bits & -bits\n                    bits ^= bit\n                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n                    if cost <= k:\n                        inherited |= bit\n            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n                      inherited):\n                return True\n            del path[here:]\n            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n            # instead would be repeating it.\n            seen |= 1 << customer\n\n        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n        # permanent wrong answer.\n        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n            refuted.add(closed)\n        del path[mark:]\n        return False\n\n    found = search(0, 0)\n    if found:\n        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n\n\ndef _apply_dominance(\n    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    opens: dict[int, int],\n    subset_rule: bool,\n    definite_move: bool,\n    *,\n    better_move: bool = False,\n    dominators: int = 4,\n    repaired: bool = False,\n    masks: list[int] | None = None,\n    full: int = 0,\n    closed: int = 0,\n    opened: int = 0,\n    k: int = 0,\n) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the dominance relations, in the order\n    definite move, subset rule, better move, each citing only candidates still\n    standing (`paper2/revised_algorithm.md` §4.4.1).\n\n    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n\n    `playable` arrives in customer index order, which is the order the better\n    move's \"earlier\" and its dominator limit refer to, as in the C.\n    `better_move` needs `masks`, `full`, `closed`, `opened` and `k`.\n    \"\"\"\n    if definite_move:\n        for cost, q in playable:\n            own = opens[q]\n            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n                # close(q, S) counts q itself; the customers q frees are the rest.\n                if repaired and not _has_definite_matching(\n                        [other for d, other in opens.items()\n                         if d != q and other & ~own == 0],\n                        opened_by_q - 1):\n                    # Chu & Stuckey's premise holds but the hereditary one does\n                    # not: a solution may avoid q (Counterexample 4.5), so q\n                    # may not stand for the others. A later q may still.\n                    continue\n                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n                # branch can go.\n                return [(cost, q)]\n\n    kept = _subset_survivors(playable, opens) if subset_rule else playable\n    if better_move and len(kept) > 1:\n        kept = _better_move_pass(kept, masks, full, closed, opened, k,\n                                 dominators, repaired)\n    return kept\n\n\ndef _subset_survivors(\n    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    opens: dict[int, int],\n) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"The subset rule with its index tie-break, dominators from every remaining customer.\"\"\"\n    kept = []\n    for cost, q in playable:\n        own = opens[q]\n        dominated = any(\n            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n        )\n        if not dominated:\n            kept.append((cost, q))\n    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n    return kept or playable\n\n\ndef _better_move_pass(\n    kept: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    masks: list[int],\n    full: int,\n    closed: int,\n    opened: int,\n    k: int,\n    dominators: int,\n    repaired: bool,\n) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"Theorem 2 over the subset survivors, the C's `better_move_pass`.\n\n    `r` goes if an earlier survivor `q`, among the first `dominators` (all if\n    0), has premise 3, `|(O(S ∪ {r}) ∪ N[q]) − (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k`, and premise 4,\n    `open' ≤ close'` with `close'` counting the `d ∉ S ∪ {r}` with\n    `∅ ≠ N[d] − O(S ∪ {r}) ⊆ N[q] − O(S ∪ {r})`: Theorem 1's premise for `q`\n    at the child `cl(S ∪ {r})` (Lemma 4.12). With `repaired`, also the\n    matching test at that child, which makes the premise `IsRepairedBetter`.\n    Only an earlier candidate may cite, so the first always survives.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = len(kept)\n    limit = dominators if 0 < dominators < count else count\n    survivors = []\n    for ri, item in enumerate(kept):\n        r = item[1]\n        closed_r = closed | (1 << r)\n        opened_r = opened | masks[r]\n        remaining_r = full & ~closed_r\n        pruned = False\n        for qi in range(min(limit, ri)):\n            q = kept[qi][1]\n            if ((opened_r | masks[q]) & ~closed_r).bit_count() > k:       # premise 3\n                continue\n            own = masks[q] & ~opened_r\n            opened_by = own.bit_count()\n            freed = []\n            closed_by = 0\n            bits = remaining_r\n            while bits:\n                bit = bits & -bits\n                bits ^= bit\n                d = bit.bit_length() - 1\n                left = masks[d] & ~opened_r\n                # Customers r finishes alone are free in the child and are not\n                # stacks q closes (Bug A, reports/better_move_bug.md §7).\n                if left and left & ~own == 0:\n                    closed_by += 1\n                    if d != q:\n                        freed.append(left)\n            if closed_by < opened_by:                                      # premise 4\n                continue\n            if repaired and not _has_definite_matching(freed, opened_by - 1):\n                continue\n            pruned = True\n            break\n        if not pruned:\n            survivors.append(item)\n    return survivors\n\n\ndef _has_definite_matching(freed: list[int], need: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether `need` of the sets in `freed` can be matched to distinct members.\n\n    `freed` holds `o(d, S)` for each customer `d ≠ q` that closing `q` frees, as\n    stack bitmasks; `need` is `open(q, S) − 1`. By Theorem 4.8\n    (`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`) the maximum matching\n    reaching `need` is exactly `q` being hereditarily definite at `S`. Kuhn's\n    augmenting paths, stopping as soon as `need` edges are matched; the sets\n    are subsets of `o(q, S)`, so the stack side is small.\n    \"\"\"\n    if need <= 0:\n        return True\n    if len(freed) < need:\n        return False\n    owner: dict[int, int] = {}          # stack -> index into freed\n\n    def augment(i: int, visited: set[int]) -> bool:\n        bits = freed[i]\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            if bit in visited:\n                continue\n            visited.add(bit)\n            if bit not in owner or augment(owner[bit], visited):\n                owner[bit] = i\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    matched = 0\n    for i in range(len(freed)):\n        if augment(i, set()):\n            matched += 1\n            if matched >= need:\n                return True\n    return False\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Solution:\n    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n\n    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n\n    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    value: int\n    order: list[int]\n    proof: str\n    nodes: int\n    seconds: float\n\n    @property\n    def proved(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self.proof)\n\n\ndef solve(\n    instance: MOSPInstance,\n    *,\n    upper: int | None = None,\n    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n    lower: int = 0,\n    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n    time_budget: float | None = None,\n    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n    **kwargs: object,\n) -> Solution:\n    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n\n    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n\n    Args:\n        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n            descent with `proved` false.\n        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n\n    Returns:\n        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    nodes = 0\n\n    if upper is None:\n        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n    else:\n        value = upper\n        order = []\n\n    if value <= lower:\n        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n    k = value - 1\n    while k >= lower:\n        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n        nodes += answer.nodes\n\n        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n            # project has had to retract before.\n            if value == k + 1:\n                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n                                time.monotonic() - started)\n            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n        found = answer.order\n        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n        if achieved < value:\n            value, order = achieved, found\n            if on_improve is not None:\n                on_improve(value, order)\n        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n\n    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n\ndef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n    return sorted(\n        active,\n        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n    )\n","numLines":725,"startLine":1,"totalLines":725}}}
