{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfejEbdsFgQ94LgU7jNm4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 05 is done: refutations by the customer search under the repaired rules now come with a certificate that a separate checker verifies. On the corpus at 9–75 customers, 6,276 of 6,286 verify and none is rejected. The write-up is `paper2/certificates.md`, and the gate passes.\n\n**What was built**\n- **The emitter** (`learning/search_certificate.py`) has a new `repaired_rules=True` mode. Each definite-move and better-move step now records its matching, the witness the repaired premise asks for. For the better move, that matching is at the child state.\n  - A new `start` option begins the search from a given set of closed customers instead of from empty. The certificate then only claims that no solution extends that set.\n  - The old defaults are unchanged, so the earlier certificate numbers and tests still stand.\n  - It visits exactly the tree the repaired search visits: same answer and node count on 2,463 refutations of random instances, and the same as the C search (memo off) on 968 decisions.\n- **The checker** (`paper2/certificate_check.py`) uses only the standard library and imports nothing from the repository; a test enforces this. It recomputes every premise from the 0/1 matrix and checks each matching edge by edge. It rejects any definite or better step that has no matching, i.e. any step under the published rules.\n\n**Results**, with a 60-second limit per certificate on 4 cores:\n\n| customers | `default` verified | `csearch` verified | rejected |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 9–40 | 6,135 of 6,135 | 6,135 of 6,135 | 0 |\n| 41–75 | 141 of 151 | 140 of 151 | 0 |\n\n- The 141 at 41–75 are the same instances the earlier published-rule certificates verified.\n- The `csearch` shortfall is `Random-75-75-4-1_0`, which ran past 60 s; `default` finished it in 46 s.\n- Ten 75-customer instances did not finish under either configuration: SP3, SP3_0, `Random-75-75-2-*` and three of `Random-75-75-4-*`.\n- Size and speed (`csearch`): 35.5 MB gzipped in total, checked in 147 s. The largest certificate is 11.6 MB, and the slowest check takes 68 s.\n\n**Hand-built failures**\n- **`DEFINITE_CEX`:** on both counterexample graphs, starting from {2}, the published rules produce a complete \"no solution\" tree, which is false.\n  - The earlier checker, which tests the published premises, accepts it.\n  - The new checker rejects it.\n  - It still rejects it when the forged step carries the largest matching that exists: 1 edge, where 2 are needed.\n  - The repaired emitter, run from {2}, finds a solution of cost at most k.\n- **Corrupted matchings:** seven single corruptions of the Warwick 877 certificate are all rejected, as are five tree corruptions and the rule-order cycle bug.\n\n**Proposed, not built: a C emitter.** It would write a binary stream of about 10 bytes per node, with the memo turned off, and one certificate per task through the root split. A 125 × 125 refutation would then be about 250 GB: checkable in principle, but not practical to ship.\n\n**Not formalised:** each rule's soundness is a Lean theorem, but the checker's overall acceptance condition is argued on paper (`certificates.md` §2), not stated in Lean.\n\n**Other changes:** I added pointers in `revised_algorithm.md` §4.6.3 and `plan.md`, plus 20 tests in `tests/test_certificate_check.py`. PROGRESS.md now reads 5/9 and item 05 is marked done. Nothing is committed. The split run's log has no `!!!` line as of 06:10."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2423,"cache_read_input_tokens":199572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2423,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":737,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"par